Chase`, `providerOrder: deepseek`, `deepseek.configured: true`, `backend/.env`, `INFOPLIST_KEY_FLASHPLAY_BACKEND_BASE_URL`, `TaskStore`, `flashplay-backend`\n  - desc: Search here first for exact `cwd=/Users/vincet/Documents/aiglass/aigame` work on local DeepSeek-backed generation, simulator-visible validation, and the later deployment-readiness checklist.\n  - learnings: the freshest split is local backend proof vs generated Cloud Run guidance. Confirm simulator state after relaunch, and treat Cloud Run steps as checklist-only until command and endpoint execution are shown.\n\n- FlashPlay account deletion and guest-reset verification: `Account deletion`, `Continue as Guest`, `Generated`, `Favorites`, `2048`, `24Points`, `Cat Jump`, `language selection`, `device id`\n  - desc: Use this topic for the `Me`-tab deletion/reset workflow: where the control appears, what backend/local data are supposed to clear, and how the generated/favorites lists should look after guest reset or Firestore-backed deletion.\n  - learnings: verify the actual post-delete lists in Simulator. The useful distinction is account-scoped cards vs intentionally retained local settings, not just whether the API route exists.\n\n### workflow:flashplay-gaaigame-mobile-generation-debug\n\n#### 2026-05-15\n\n- FlashPlay/GaAiGame `Cat Catch` MiniMax failure isolation: `Could not connect to the server`, `minimax:minimax_api_error:2049:invalid api key`, `minimax:minimax_chat_failed:401`, `127.0.0.1:18900`, `backend/.env`\n  - desc: Search here first for the iPhone Simulator tiny-game generator debugging loop that preceded the May 16 DeepSeek pass: online-backend vs local-backend routing, MiniMax key/endpoint diagnosis, and exact simulator repro steps from the `Cat Catch` create screen.\n  - learnings: the main decision trigger is the error transition. If the failure changes from connection to provider-key to provider-auth, the routing changed and the remaining bug is more specific than “backend broken.”\n\n### /Users/vincet/Documents/New project/KidCreatorMac\n\n#### 2026-05-15\n\n- KidCreatorMac scaffold, export surface, provider debugging, and packaging proof: `PM skill;`, `DeepSeekClient.swift`, `ArtifactExporter.swift`, `MiniMax-M2.7`, `presentation.pptx`, `Kid-Creator-0.1.0.dmg`, `/private/tmp/kidcreator-no-codex-home`\n  - desc: Use this topic for the May 15 SwiftUI/macOS child-friendly Codex-like assistant app: product architecture, independent app scaffold, artifact export, floating-composer UX, provider/keychain work, and later PPT/DMG packaging verification.\n  - learnings: preserve the independent-app boundary, validate through the live `Kid Creator` window, and treat “works on a new computer” as part of done.\n\n### workflow:debot-bsc-signal-monitor-and-token-cross-check\n\n#### 2026-05-14\n\n- BSC DeBot monitor, resident script, and contract cross-check: `chain=bsc&tab=signal`, `run-debot-bsc-desktop-monitor.sh`, `debot-bsc-monitor.mjs`, `scanned=0`, `ERR_NETWORK_CHANGED`, `自由猫`\n  - desc: Use this topic for the BSC-specific monitoring workflow: live DeBot dashboard watching, X/holder-analysis cross-checking, resident desktop monitor setup, and proxy-aware troubleshooting.\n  - learnings: preserve both the process handle and the contract handle. `scanned=0` and proxy state belong in the same diagnostic picture as dashboard failures.\n\n### /Users/vincet/Desktop/aigame2\n\n#### 2026-05-14\n\n- `aigame2` one-track continuation and Aimuse simulator recovery: `Night Train Downtempo`, `fetch failed`, `manifest unchanged`, `86 tracks`, `item 32 failed`, `continue item 33`, `Aimus.app/seed_music`, `com.museplay.ios`\n  - desc: Search here for the latest `cwd=/Users/vincet/Desktop/aigame2` recovery state: manifest-aware one-track continuation after `fetch failed`, plus the adjacent Aimuse simulator relaunch/check that confirmed the music UI and generated-song list actually came back.\n  - learnings: if a batch item fails, keep the exact title, verify no leftover process or manifest write, then move forward. When restart/build status is uncertain, confirm the live simulator list/detail view and counters instead of trusting tool output alone.\n\n### Older Memory Topics\n\n#### workflow:ai-skill-stack-research\n\n- Local PM/OKR and presentation skill lookup in Codex: `measure-okr-grader`, `utility-update-pm-skills`, `Presentations`, `utility-slideshow-creator`, `/Users/vincet/Documents/skills/DESIGN.md`\n  - desc: Use this topic for exact installed PM/presentation skill names and source paths; search here before answering any local-skill lookup request.\n\n#### /Users/vincet/Documents/aiglass/godot-codex-lab\n\n- Godot Codex Lab ideation and five-map progression: `3D multi-map teleport/exploration game`, `5 maps`, `HUD x / 5`, `final-door unlock`, `Attempting to use a shader that requires tangents`\n  - desc: Covers the earlier 3D Godot lab thread: tangent-warning/import context plus the later concrete five-map progression/gating plan in this checkout.\n\n#### workflow:flashplay-ios-account-deletion-verification\n\n- FlashPlay account deletion simulator verification: `FlashPlay`, `cd0423a Add account deletion flow`, `f75bdca Add Firestore-backed account deletion`, `Me`, `Account deletion`, `Continue as Guest`\n  - desc: Covers `Me`-tab verification of guest-vs-signed-in state, backend/local cleanup semantics, and the later May 16 generated/favorites reset checks in Simulator.\n\n#### workflow:debot-solana-signal-monitor-validation\n\n- DeBot monitor dry run, Telegram validation, and proxy troubleshooting: `.env.debot-monitor`, `scripts/run-debot-monitor.sh`, `DEBOT_DRY_RUN=1`, `Vinto sats`, `暂无数据`, `自由猫`\n  - desc: Covers the Solana/ETH monitor-verification workflow: Telegram delivery, dry-run command/process checks, dashboard watching, and proxy-aware troubleshooting.\n\n#### workflow:sol-eth-hot-project-monitoring-and-telegram-delivery\n\n- SOL/ETH hot-project monitoring to Telegram delivery: `eth-sol-p0-new-projects-30m-watch`, `Vinto sats`, `上线 30 分钟内优质候选`, `project name + CA`, `RKC/SOL`, `WORLDCUP/SOL`\n  - desc: Covers the Chinese monitoring prompts, the every-30-minutes watch update, BotFather setup around `@meme1188_bot`, Telegram delivery, and CA-first formatting pivots.\n\n#### workflow:visual-asset-generation-and-web3d-handoff\n\n- Seedance/Jimeng image-to-video handoff: `Seedance 2.0`, `Jimeng`, `Downloads/test`, `16:9`, `480p`, `5-second duration`, `Vinto sats`\n  - desc: Covers the short browser clip-generation workflow from a local Finder asset into Telegram handoff and later Blender/media triage.\n\n#### workflow:browser-image-to-3d-and-blender-handoff\n\n- Browser image-to-3D platform evaluation for Blender handoff: `Tripo`, `Meshy`, `Hi3D`, `Neural4D`, `bind skeleton`, `biped`, `glb`, `Nano-Banana-Pro`\n  - desc: Covers same-image comparison across tools, export/licensing/credit checks, multi-view/edit settings, and downstream Blender suitability.\n\n#### workflow:eth-heat-scan-and-uniswap-v4-pool-monitoring\n\n- ETH heat scan to contract/pool monitoring: `latest heat on Twitter and the ETH chain`, `LOOP/ETH`, `SHIT/ETH`, `PONZI`, `0xce4a5d11fad8f04d76eb65a588dc6a2e2a734255`\n  - desc: Covers the ETH market-monitoring thread that pivots from hot-pair scanning into exact contract/pool verification and watch conditions.\n\n#### /Users/vincet/Downloads/blender角色场景合集\n\n- Blender asset triage and fast-render pivot: `A2_Alt_Patreon完整骨骼.blend`, `Mercy Pinup 2.blend`, `render_blend_file_fast.py`, `mercy_pinup_render.png`\n  - desc: Covers archive extraction, rig/reference inspection, render attempts, and the pivot from stalled automation to a faster usable render route.\n\n#### workflow:codexmint-eth-nft-planning\n\n- Ferryman research and CodexMint scoping: `Ferryman`, `mint ended`, `CodexMint`, `wallet confirms`, `nft-mint-builder`, `codexmint-generator.html`\n  - desc: Covers Ferryman reconnaissance, mint-assistant scoping, the explicit wallet-confirmation boundary, and the separate standalone NFT-generator artifact.\n========= MEMORY_SUMMARY ENDS =========\n\nWhen memory is likely relevant, start with the quick memory pass above before\ndeep repo exploration.\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"<collaboration_mode># Collaboration Mode: Default\n\nYou are now in Default mode. Any previous instructions for other modes (e.g. Plan mode) are no longer active.\n\nYour active mode changes only when new developer instructions with a different `<collaboration_mode>...</collaboration_mode>` change it; user requests or tool descriptions do not change mode by themselves. Known mode names are Default and Plan.\n\n## request_user_input availability\n\nUse the `request_user_input` tool only when it is listed in the available tools for this turn.\n\nIn Default mode, strongly prefer making reasonable assumptions and executing the user's request rather than stopping to ask questions. If you absolutely must ask a question because the answer cannot be discovered from local context and a reasonable assumption would be risky, ask the user directly with a concise plain-text question. Never write a multiple choice question as a textual assistant message.\n</collaboration_mode>"},{"type":"input_text","text":"<apps_instructions>\n## Apps (Connectors)\nApps (Connectors) can be explicitly triggered in user messages in the format `[$app-name](app://{connector_id})`. Apps can also be implicitly triggered as long as the context suggests usage of available apps.\nAn app is equivalent to a set of MCP tools within the `codex_apps` MCP.\nAn installed app's MCP tools are either provided to you already, or can be lazy-loaded through the `tool_search` tool. If `tool_search` is available, the apps that are searchable by `tools_search` will be listed by it.\nDo not additionally call list_mcp_resources or list_mcp_resource_templates for apps.\n</apps_instructions>"},{"type":"input_text","text":"<skills_instructions>\n## Skills\nA skill is a set of local instructions to follow that is stored in a `SKILL.md` file. Below is the list of skills that can be used. Each entry includes a name, description, and a short path that can be expanded into an absolute path using the skill roots table.\n### Skill roots\n- `r0` = `/Users/vincet/.codex/skills`\n- `r1` = `/Users/vincet/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-bundled`\n- `r2` = `/Users/vincet/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated/build-ios-apps/63976030/skills`\n- `r3` = `/Users/vincet/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated/build-macos-apps/63976030/skills`\n- `r4` = `/Users/vincet/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated/build-web-apps/63976030/skills`\n- `r5` = `/Users/vincet/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated/circleci/63976030/skills`\n- `r6` = `/Users/vincet/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated/cloudflare/63976030/skills`\n- `r7` = `/Users/vincet/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated`\n- `r8` = `/Users/vincet/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated/game-studio/63976030/skills`\n- `r9` = `/Users/vincet/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated/github/63976030/skills`\n- `r10` = `/Users/vincet/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated/stripe/63976030/skills`\n- `r11` = `/Users/vincet/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated/test-android-apps/63976030/skills`\n- `r12` = `/Users/vincet/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-primary-runtime`\n### Available skills\n- browser-use:browser: Browser automation for the Codex in-app browser. Use to open, navigate, inspect, test, click, type, screenshot, or verify local targets such as localhost, 127.0.0.1, ::1, file://, the current in-app browser tab, and websites shown side by side inside Codex. (file: r1/browser-use/0.1.0-alpha2/skills/browser/SKILL.md)\n- build-ios-apps:ios-app-intents: Design and implement App Intents, app entities, and App Shortcuts for iOS apps so useful actions and content are available to Shortcuts, Siri, Spotlight, widgets, controls, and other intent-driven system surfaces. Use when exposing app actions outside the UI, adding `AppEntity` and `E (file: r2/ios-app-intents/SKILL.md)\n- build-ios-apps:ios-debugger-agent: Use XcodeBuildMCP to build, run, launch, and debug the current iOS project on a booted simulator. Trigger when asked to run an iOS app, interact with the simulator UI, inspect on-screen state, capture logs/console output, or diagnose runtime behavior using XcodeBuildMCP tools. (file: r2/ios-debugger-agent/SKILL.md)\n- build-ios-apps:ios-ettrace-performance: Capture and interpret ETTrace profiles for iOS simulator apps, including symbolicated launch and runtime flamegraphs. Use when asked to profile an iOS app flow, gather simulator performance traces, identify CPU-heavy stacks, compare before/after traces, or produce flamegraph evidence  (file: r2/ios-ettrace-performance/SKILL.md)\n- build-ios-apps:ios-memgraph-leaks: Capture, inspect, compare, and root-cause iOS memory graph leaks using Apple's leaks and memgraph tools. Use when debugging leaked iOS objects, simulator memgraphs, retain-cycle suspicions, memory growth after navigation/logout/account changes, or when asked to prove an iOS leak fix (file: r2/ios-memgraph-leaks/SKILL.md)\n- build-ios-apps:swiftui-liquid-glass: Implement, review, or improve SwiftUI features using the iOS 26+ Liquid Glass API. Use when asked to adopt Liquid Glass in new SwiftUI UI, refactor an existing feature to Liquid Glass, or review Liquid Glass usage for correctness, performance, and design alignment. (file: r2/swiftui-liquid-glass/SKILL.md)\n- build-ios-apps:swiftui-performance-audit: Audit and improve SwiftUI runtime performance from code review and architecture. Use for requests to diagnose slow rendering, janky scrolling, high CPU/memory usage, excessive view updates, or layout thrash in SwiftUI apps, and to provide guidance for user-run Instruments profiling wh (file: r2/swiftui-performance-audit/SKILL.md)\n- build-ios-apps:swiftui-ui-patterns: Best practices and example-driven guidance for building SwiftUI views and components, including navigation hierarchies, custom view modifiers, and responsive layouts with stacks and grids. Use when creating or refactoring SwiftUI UI, designing tab architecture with TabView, composing  (file: r2/swiftui-ui-patterns/SKILL.md)\n- build-ios-apps:swiftui-view-refactor: Refactor and review SwiftUI view files with strong defaults for small dedicated subviews, MV-over-MVVM data flow, stable view trees, explicit dependency injection, and correct Observation usage. Use when cleaning up a SwiftUI view, splitting long bodies, removing inline actions or sid (file: r2/swiftui-view-refactor/SKILL.md)\n- build-macos-apps:appkit-interop: Decide when and how to bridge a macOS app from SwiftUI into AppKit. Use when implementing NSViewRepresentable or NSViewControllerRepresentable, accessing NSWindow or the responder chain, presenting panels, customizing menus, or handling desktop behaviors that SwiftUI does not model cl (file: r3/appkit-interop/SKILL.md)\n- build-macos-apps:build-run-debug: Build, run, and debug local macOS apps and desktop executables using shell-first Xcode and Swift workflows. Use when asked to build a Mac app, launch it, diagnose compiler or linker failures, inspect startup problems, or debug desktop-only runtime issues. (file: r3/build-run-debug/SKILL.md)\n- build-macos-apps:liquid-glass: Implement, refactor, or review modern macOS SwiftUI UI for the new design system and Liquid Glass. Use when adopting Liquid Glass, updating NavigationSplitView, toolbars, search, sheets, and controls, removing custom backgrounds that fight system materials, or building custom glass su (file: r3/liquid-glass/SKILL.md)\n- build-macos-apps:packaging-notarization: Prepare and troubleshoot packaging, signing, and notarization workflows for macOS distribution. Use when asked to archive a Mac app, validate bundle structure, reason about notarization readiness, or explain distribution-only failures. (file: r3/packaging-notarization/SKILL.md)\n- build-macos-apps:signing-entitlements: Inspect signing, entitlements, hardened runtime, and Gatekeeper issues for macOS apps. Use when asked to diagnose code signing failures, missing entitlements, sandbox problems, notarization prerequisites, or trust-policy launch errors. (file: r3/signing-entitlements/SKILL.md)\n- build-macos-apps:swiftpm-macos: Build, run, and test pure SwiftPM-based macOS packages and executables. Use when the repo is package-first, when there is no Xcode project, or when Swift package workflows are the fastest path to diagnosis. (file: r3/swiftpm-macos/SKILL.md)\n- build-macos-apps:swiftui-patterns: Best practices and example-driven guidance for building native macOS SwiftUI scenes and components, including windows, commands, toolbars, settings, split views, inspectors, menu bar extras, and keyboard-driven workflows. Use when creating or refactoring macOS SwiftUI UI, choosing sce (file: r3/swiftui-patterns/SKILL.md)\n- build-macos-apps:telemetry: Add lightweight runtime telemetry and debug instrumentation to macOS apps, then verify those events after building and running. Use when wiring `Logger` / `os.Logger`, adding log points for window/sidebar/menu-bar actions, reading runtime logs from Console or `log stream`, or confir (file: r3/telemetry/SKILL.md)\n- build-macos-apps:test-triage: Triage failing macOS tests across Xcode and SwiftPM workflows. Use when asked to run macOS tests, narrow failing scopes, explain assertion or crash failures, or separate real test regressions from setup and environment problems. (file: r3/test-triage/SKILL.md)\n- build-macos-apps:view-refactor: Refactor macOS SwiftUI views and scenes with strong defaults for small dedicated subviews, stable sidebar and selection structure, explicit command and toolbar ownership, scene-aware state, and narrow AppKit escape hatches. Use when cleaning up a macOS view file, splitting oversized (file: r3/view-refactor/SKILL.md)\n- build-macos-apps:window-management: Customize macOS 15+ SwiftUI windows and scene behavior using Window, WindowGroup, and macOS window modifiers. Use when styling or hiding window toolbars and titles, extending drag regions with WindowDragGesture, replacing window backgrounds with materials, disabling minimize or rest (file: r3/window-management/SKILL.md)\n- build-web-apps:frontend-app-builder: Use for new frontend applications, dashboards, games, creative websites, hero sections, and visually driven UI from scratch, or when the user explicitly asks for a redesign/restyle/modernization. Builds from clean, airy, high-taste, readable image-generated concept design with secti (file: r4/frontend-app-builder/SKILL.md)\n- build-web-apps:frontend-testing-debugging: Use when testing, debugging, or making targeted improvements to rendered frontend apps through the Build Web Apps or web dev plugin: local dev servers, UI regressions, interaction bugs, console errors, responsive layout, and visual QA. Check whether the Browser plugin is available a (file: r4/frontend-testing-debugging/SKILL.md)\n- build-web-apps:react-best-practices: React and Next.js performance optimization guidelines from Vercel Engineering. This skill should be used when writing, reviewing, or refactoring React/Next.js code to ensure optimal performance patterns. Triggers on tasks involving React components, Next.js pages, data fetching, bun (file: r4/react-best-practices/SKILL.md)\n- build-web-apps:shadcn: Manages shadcn components and projects — adding, searching, fixing, debugging, styling, and composing UI. Provides project context, component docs, and usage examples. Applies when working with shadcn/ui, component registries, presets, --preset codes, or any project with a components.j (file: r4/shadcn-best-practices/SKILL.md)\n- build-web-apps:stripe-best-practices: Guides Stripe integration decisions — API selection (Checkout Sessions vs PaymentIntents), Connect platform setup (Accounts v2, controller properties), billing/subscriptions, Treasury financial accounts, integration surfaces (Checkout, Payment Element), and migrating from deprecated (file: r4/stripe-best-practices/SKILL.md)\n- build-web-apps:supabase-postgres-best-practices: Postgres performance optimization and best practices from Supabase. Use this skill when writing, reviewing, or optimizing Postgres queries, schema designs, or database configurations. (file: r4/supabase-best-practices/SKILL.md)\n- chronicle: Allows you to view the user's screen as well as several hours of history. Use when the user makes a reference to their recent work, for which it'd be helpful to see the screen. This skill MUST be used whenever you need to resolve ambiguity in a user request, where the user hasn't spe (file: r0/chronicle/SKILL.md)\n- circleci:chunk: Use CircleCI Chunk for AI-assisted CI/CD work through either the Chunk web UI or the chunk-cli. Trigger this skill when users ask to set up Chunk, troubleshoot or fix failing builds with Chunk, configure Chunk environments, schedule/proactively run Chunk tasks, or use chunk-cli comm (file: r5/chunk/SKILL.md)\n- circleci:circleci-builds: Diagnose and fix failing CircleCI builds quickly and safely. Use when users ask to investigate failed CircleCI jobs, triage flaky pipelines, identify root causes from logs, and implement minimal fixes in configuration, test setup, or build-related code paths. (file: r5/builds/SKILL.md)\n- circleci:circleci-cli: Operate and troubleshoot CircleCI using the CircleCI CLI. Use when users ask to authenticate CLI access, inspect pipeline/workflow/job status, validate configuration locally, rerun pipelines/jobs, trigger pipelines, or gather actionable diagnostics from CLI outputs. (file: r5/cli/SKILL.md)\n- circleci:circleci-config: Optimize CircleCI configuration for speed, reliability, and maintainability. Use when users ask to improve `.circleci/config.yml`, reduce CI runtime, tune caching/workspaces/parallelism, remove pipeline waste, or fix flaky pipeline behavior caused by configuration choices. (file: r5/config/SKILL.md)\n- cloudflare:agents-sdk: Build AI agents on Cloudflare Workers using the Agents SDK. Load when creating stateful agents, durable workflows, real-time WebSocket apps, scheduled tasks, MCP servers, or chat applications. Covers Agent class, state management, callable RPC, Workflows integration, and React hooks (file: r6/agents-sdk/SKILL.md)\n- cloudflare:building-ai-agent-on-cloudflare: Builds AI agents on Cloudflare using the Agents SDK with state management, real-time WebSockets, scheduled tasks, tool integration, and chat capabilities. Generates production-ready agent code deployed to Workers. Use when: user wants to \"build an agent\", \"AI agent\", \"chat agent\", \"st (file: r6/building-ai-agent-on-cloudflare/SKILL.md)\n- cloudflare:building-mcp-server-on-cloudflare: Builds remote MCP (Model Context Protocol) servers on Cloudflare Workers with tools, OAuth authentication, and production deployment. Generates server code, configures auth providers, and deploys to Workers. Use when: user wants to \"build MCP server\", \"create MCP tools\", \"remote MCP\", (file: r6/building-mcp-server-on-cloudflare/SKILL.md)\n- cloudflare:cloudflare: Comprehensive Cloudflare platform skill covering Workers, Pages, storage (KV, D1, R2), AI (Workers AI, Vectorize, Agents SDK), networking (Tunnel, Spectrum), security (WAF, DDoS), and infrastructure-as-code (Terraform, Pulumi). Use for any Cloudflare development task. Biases towards (file: r6/cloudflare/SKILL.md)\n- cloudflare:durable-objects: Create and review Cloudflare Durable Objects. Use when building stateful coordination (chat rooms, multiplayer games, booking systems), implementing RPC methods, SQLite storage, alarms, WebSockets, or reviewing DO code for best practices. Covers Workers integration, wrangler config, a (file: r6/durable-objects/SKILL.md)\n- cloudflare:sandbox-sdk: Build sandboxed applications for secure code execution. Load when building AI code execution, code interpreters, CI/CD systems, interactive dev environments, or executing untrusted code. Covers Sandbox SDK lifecycle, commands, files, code interpreter, and preview URLs. Biases towards  (file: r6/sandbox-sdk/SKILL.md)\n- cloudflare:web-perf: Analyzes web performance using Chrome DevTools MCP. Measures Core Web Vitals (FCP, LCP, TBT, CLS, Speed Index), identifies render-blocking resources, network dependency chains, layout shifts, caching issues, and accessibility gaps. Use when asked to audit, profile, debug, or optimiz (file: r6/web-perf/SKILL.md)\n- cloudflare:workers-best-practices: Reviews and authors Cloudflare Workers code against production best practices. Load when writing new Workers, reviewing Worker code, configuring wrangler.jsonc, or checking for common Workers anti-patterns (streaming, floating promises, global state, secrets, bindings, observability (file: r6/workers-best-practices/SKILL.md)\n- cloudflare:wrangler: Cloudflare Workers CLI for deploying, developing, and managing Workers, KV, R2, D1, Vectorize, Hyperdrive, Workers AI, Containers, Queues, Workflows, Pipelines, and Secrets Store. Load before running wrangler commands to ensure correct syntax and best practices. Biases towards retri (file: r6/wrangler/SKILL.md)\n- coderabbit:code-review: Reviews code changes using CodeRabbit AI. Use when user asks for code review, PR feedback, code quality checks, security issues, or requests fix-review cycles. (file: r7/coderabbit/63976030/skills/coderabbit-review/SKILL.md)\n- computer-use:computer-use: Control local Mac apps through Computer Use. Use for tasks that require reading or operating app UI by clicking, typing, scrolling, dragging, pressing keys, or setting values. (file: r1/computer-use/1.0.780/skills/computer-use/SKILL.md)\n- documents:documents: Create, edit, redline, and comment on `.docx`, Word, and Google Docs-targeted document artifacts inside the container, with a strict render-and-verify workflow. Use `render_docx.py` to generate page PNGs (and optional PDF) for visual QA, then iterate until layout is flawless before  (file: r12/documents/26.515.10143/skills/documents/SKILL.md)\n- game-studio:game-playtest: Run browser-game playtests and frontend QA. Use when the user asks for smoke tests, screenshot-based verification, browser automation, HUD or overlay review, or structured issue-finding in a browser game. (file: r8/game-playtest/SKILL.md)\n- game-studio:game-studio: Route early browser-game work. Use when the user needs stack selection and workflow planning across design, implementation, assets, and playtesting before moving to a specialist skill. (file: r8/game-studio/SKILL.md)\n- game-studio:game-ui-frontend: Design UI surfaces for browser games. Use when the user asks for HUDs, menus, overlays, responsive layouts, or visual direction that must protect the playfield. (file: r8/game-ui-frontend/SKILL.md)\n- game-studio:phaser-2d-game: Implement 2D browser games with Phaser. Use when the user wants a Phaser, TypeScript, and Vite stack for scenes, gameplay systems, cameras, sprite animation, and DOM-overlay HUD patterns. (file: r8/phaser-2d-game/SKILL.md)\n- game-studio:react-three-fiber-game: Build React-hosted 3D browser games with React Three Fiber. Use when the user wants pmndrs-based scene composition, shared React state, and 3D HUD integration inside a React app. (file: r8/react-three-fiber-game/SKILL.md)\n- game-studio:sprite-pipeline: Generate and normalize 2D sprite animations. Use when the user asks for full-strip generation from approved source frames, consistent anchor and scale normalization, or preview assets for browser-game animation. (file: r8/sprite-pipeline/SKILL.md)\n- game-studio:three-webgl-game: Implement browser-game runtimes with plain Three.js. Use when the user wants imperative scene control in TypeScript or Vite with GLB assets, loaders, physics, and low-level WebGL debugging. (file: r8/three-webgl-game/SKILL.md)\n- game-studio:web-3d-asset-pipeline: Prepare and optimize browser-game 3D assets. Use when the user asks for GLB or glTF shipping work, including Blender cleanup and export, collision or LOD setup, compression, texture packaging, and runtime validation. (file: r8/web-3d-asset-pipeline/SKILL.md)\n- game-studio:web-game-foundations: Set browser-game architecture before implementation. Use when the user needs engine choice, simulation and render boundaries, input model, asset organization, or save/debug/performance strategy. (file: r8/web-game-foundations/SKILL.md)\n- github:gh-address-comments: Address actionable GitHub pull request review feedback. Use when the user wants to inspect unresolved review threads, requested changes, or inline review comments on a PR, then implement selected fixes. Use the GitHub app for PR metadata and flat comment reads, and use the bundled Gra (file: r9/gh-address-comments/SKILL.md)\n- github:gh-fix-ci: Use when a user asks to debug or fix failing GitHub PR checks that run in GitHub Actions. Use the GitHub app from this plugin for PR metadata and patch context, and use `gh` for Actions check and log inspection before implementing any approved fix. (file: r9/gh-fix-ci/SKILL.md)\n- github:github: Triage and orient GitHub repository, pull request, and issue work through the connected GitHub app. Use when the user asks for general GitHub help, wants PR or issue summaries, or needs repository context before choosing a more specific GitHub workflow. (file: r9/github/SKILL.md)\n- github:yeet: Publish local changes to GitHub by confirming scope, committing intentionally, pushing the branch, and opening a draft PR through the GitHub app from this plugin, with `gh` used only as a fallback where connector coverage is insufficient. (file: r9/yeet/SKILL.md)\n- imagegen: Generate or edit raster images when a task needs AI-created bitmap visuals such as character cards, illustrations, textures, sprites, mockups, photos, or transparent-background cutouts. Use when creating new images, transforming existing images, or deriving visual variants from ref (file: r0/imagegen/SKILL.md)\n- presentations:Presentations: Build premium editorial analytics presentation decks for PowerPoint and Google Slides with artifact-tool presentation JSX, using ruthless narrative editing, chart-first storytelling, rendered critique, platform-specific delivery rules, and iteration until the output beats the refere (file: r12/presentations/26.515.10143/skills/presentations/SKILL.md)\n- remotion-best-practices: Best practices for Remotion - Video creation in React (file: r0/remotion/SKILL.md)\n- spreadsheets:Spreadsheets: Use this skill when a user requests to create, modify, analyze, visualize, or work with spreadsheet files (`.xlsx`, `.xls`, `.csv`, `.tsv`) or Google Sheets-targeted spreadsheet artifacts with formulas, formatting, charts, tables, and recalculation. (file: r12/spreadsheets/26.515.10143/skills/spreadsheets/SKILL.md)\n- stripe:stripe-best-practices: Guides Stripe integration decisions — API selection (Checkout Sessions vs PaymentIntents), Connect platform setup (Accounts v2, controller properties), billing/subscriptions, Treasury financial accounts, integration surfaces (Checkout, Payment Element), and migrating from deprecate (file: r10/stripe-best-practices/SKILL.md)\n- stripe:upgrade-stripe: Guide for upgrading Stripe API versions and SDKs (file: r10/upgrade-stripe/SKILL.md)\n- test-android-apps:android-emulator-qa: Use when validating Android feature flows in an emulator with adb-driven launch, input, UI-tree inspection, screenshots, and logcat capture. (file: r11/android-emulator-qa/SKILL.md)\n- test-android-apps:android-performance: Gather and interpret Android performance evidence on an adb target using Simpleperf CPU profiles, Perfetto or Compose traces, gfxinfo frame data, dumpsys meminfo snapshots, Java heap dumps, and native allocation traces. Use when asked to profile an Android app flow, find CPU-heavy fun (file: r11/android-performance/SKILL.md)\n### How to use skills\n- Discovery: The list above is the skills available in this session (name + description + short path). Skill bodies live on disk at the listed paths after expanding the matching alias from `### Skill roots`.\n- Trigger rules: If the user names a skill (with `$SkillName` or plain text) OR the task clearly matches a skill's description shown above, you must use that skill for that turn. Multiple mentions mean use them all. Do not carry skills across turns unless re-mentioned.\n- Missing/blocked: If a named skill isn't in the list or the path can't be read, say so briefly and continue with the best fallback.\n- How to use a skill (progressive disclosure):\n  1) After deciding to use a skill, expand the listed short `path` with the matching alias from `### Skill roots`, then open its `SKILL.md`. Read only enough to follow the workflow.\n  2) When `SKILL.md` references relative paths (e.g., `scripts/foo.py`), resolve them relative to the directory containing that expanded `SKILL.md` first, and only consider other paths if needed.\n  3) If `SKILL.md` points to extra folders such as `references/`, load only the specific files needed for the request; don't bulk-load everything.\n  4) If `scripts/` exist, prefer running or patching them instead of retyping large code blocks.\n  5) If `assets/` or templates exist, reuse them instead of recreating from scratch.\n- Coordination and sequencing:\n  - If multiple skills apply, choose the minimal set that covers the request and state the order you'll use them.\n  - Announce which skill(s) you're using and why (one short line). If you skip an obvious skill, say why.\n- Context hygiene:\n  - Keep context small: summarize long sections instead of pasting them; only load extra files when needed.\n  - Avoid deep reference-chasing: prefer opening only files directly linked from `SKILL.md` unless you're blocked.\n  - When variants exist (frameworks, providers, domains), pick only the relevant reference file(s) and note that choice.\n- Safety and fallback: If a skill can't be applied cleanly (missing files, unclear instructions), state the issue, pick the next-best approach, and continue.\n</skills_instructions>"},{"type":"input_text","text":"<plugins_instructions>\n## Plugins\nA plugin is a local bundle of skills, MCP servers, and apps. Below is the list of plugins that are enabled and available in this session.\n### Available plugins\n- `Browser`: Browser / browser-use plugin Aliases: @browser, @browser-use, browser-use, Browser, in-app browser. Use Browser, the Codex in-app browser, when the user asks to open, inspect, navigate, test, click, type, or screenshot local web targets such as localhost, 127.0.0.1, ::1, file:// URLs, or the current in-app browser tab. After significant frontend changes to a local app, use Browser to open the relevant local target when it is known or obvious, unless the user asks for another browser tool. For requests like \"open localhost:3000\" or \"open to localhost:4000\", navigate the in-app browser to http://localhost:3000 or http://localhost:4000. Do not satisfy explicit @browser or @browser-use requests with macOS `open`, shell commands, or generic web browsing unless the user asks for another browser tool or approves a fallback.\n- `Build iOS Apps`: Build iOS apps with workflows for App Intents, SwiftUI UI work, refactors, performance profiling, leak investigation, and simulator debugging.\n- `Build macOS Apps`: Build, run, test, debug, instrument, and implement local macOS apps using Xcode, SwiftUI, AppKit interop, unified logging, and shell-first desktop workflows.\n- `Build Web Apps`: Build web apps with frontend asset design, browser testing, UI components, payments, and database guidance.\n- `CircleCI`: Build, test, and deploy any application\n- `Cloudflare`: Cloudflare platform plugin with curated skills for Workers, Wrangler, and Agents SDK plus the official Cloudflare API MCP server.\n- `CodeRabbit`: AI-powered code review in Codex, powered by CodeRabbit.\n- `Computer Use`: Control desktop apps on macOS from Codex through Computer Use.\n- `Documents`: Create and edit document artifacts in Codex, including Word files and Google Docs.\n- `Game Studio`: Design, prototype, and ship browser games with guided 2D and 3D workflows, asset pipelines, and playtesting support.\n- `GitHub`: Inspect repositories, triage pull requests and issues, debug CI, and publish changes through a hybrid GitHub connector and CLI workflow.\n- `Presentations`: Create, edit, render, verify, and export presentation slide decks. Use when Codex needs to build or modify a deck, slidedeck, presentation deck, slide deck, slides, PowerPoint, Google Slides, PPT, PPTX, .ppt, or .pptx file.\n- `Spreadsheets`: Create, edit, analyze, visualize, render, and export spreadsheets or Google Sheets-ready workbooks in Codex.\n- `Stripe`: Payments and business tools\n- `Test Android Apps`: Test Android apps with emulator workflows for reproduction, screenshots, UI inspection, log capture, and performance profiling.\n### How to use plugins\n- Discovery: The list above is the plugins available in this session.\n- Skill naming: If a plugin contributes skills, those skill entries are prefixed with `plugin_name:` in the Skills list.\n- Trigger rules: If the user explicitly names a plugin, prefer capabilities associated with that plugin for that turn.\n- Relationship to capabilities: Plugins are not invoked directly. Use their underlying skills, MCP tools, and app tools to help solve the task.\n- Preference: When a relevant plugin is available, prefer using capabilities associated with that plugin over standalone capabilities that provide similar functionality.\n- Missing/blocked: If the user requests a plugin that is not listed above, or the plugin does not have relevant callable capabilities for the task, say so briefly and continue with the best fallback.\n</plugins_instructions>"}]},{"type":"message","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"<environment_context>\n  <cwd>/Users/vincet/Documents/aiglass</cwd>\n  <shell>zsh</shell>\n  <current_date>2026-05-16</current_date>\n  <timezone>Asia/Taipei</timezone>\n</environment_context>"}]},{"type":"message","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"ai challenge 板块，ai角色血量还是按照正常收到攻击到会倒地；摄像头按照玩家角色为准；ai角色头上亮红色的光点。以ai角色通关为准进入下一个关卡。\n"}]},{"type":"compaction","encrypted_content":"gAAAAABqCIsYaabreoDVV29TP1H-PMqoFuYwUZR_3HWSliFKWZcPGa85JfqsvjsF4zdtMMlBfJR0P6XOfS78TNPRRMEVvZXqMryJVnVfNBqi9ZqIQ9NWSUN_airQRgPlus1zvmkrggYqWMYvVWLJorpo-op_5ntPgay9h83nqkK-ANhykCVScMA_9S4tXR4Ob2JAjzElDLIx64_gsEaHCbLVgOW8KKaMsIh9vIqe3X873r0zav-JAUvuQIvEA3PpBp9Sa-w1SIkyB7NE_Z5KMhqEEscA93eYf1E63L2n3EZrv0OTYc_nZI1rJei-g9KUABunaCe0ELLGMRb19sotyry4G-SHnmM8hYly8JRdoG-Nfxonzo6AK_rCZxUXwKCATnXAnnEUAqOldhIJEiL94ypcBhAtlPf0c_AjFV_wPQ0AFe8BUEGIq-Ot6UYJuQZ3nKLCfbaD7zLeArct2SftiDtjDTejFVfnQNgXfi_6TtxG-_txHPGyEPXrKRauMZjkPwe_OuizQVSKzAV8n2Rryip9ZMjGJ4RBpIo0IVHHB04XYE52FPz3m7eEMmnoHu7XQuUmhIzuAwGbIqWOorbHb9WzFeFSmAkcWOrVvIPvByCaGYaXb1PONrJx26PU7WhJZYdfYcxzTYsBv4x__xGkjYCnRpNqh6czZRqcd3x7abryQSwrEj8KS81nL_T0oUCKAPD4P8hHk4F3HlfkvRKs4FBF4WkkwAf8eT_L_DSN86zrFmFFMt2uQQMFhsN4I7tbs7Q7FNmZPtFqVF2ZFRR7R3E6p-jOPHq3-2kYJXEipgko6URIJlFQvMmSUcKfcdhqRNI-ekYtIIMOUmzzFCGpe-yj384oSEM1cZOnxN1REGAfnAVxi3y5wePpJFOMWpRnePYBKAwLMUZQzFtoj85dyj6e-rr3q-1fJdd8z7XM6y5O4SUgD_LG9r8h912T59fBuMZTYrgIVhATPwzy-OhXIk5Z5bsGJ0P0tqHu-G_bbueU6OQjvE4H4HJG_S-qgdyYNYoBDnU9b1DrLzg3MQV4xWFih-qy-PotW_3AVXVNibBM-03EwbgvoR2giqK25S_rMEaltGFnRY1qh9wInpD5HkzB_XaNs6h1aA-rLE1EFsuepVR53YOFgqn5iimnUbtPki6ICj3W6S5x8ATXlqw5QKdQtSc99CelOQlB2KpiUja7vpw3EIcBWQeFKIg1HMq8zPExy0JKwz32pag2R8tDIhpbt9CLPBT63i5Fv4rqq3setgqYfoIk4YbC2QDI3XmH1lvIwUf8ypemGHFIpAgOlhWvxx7M8ybj0oBwmWI2BCb6P921JRv_caWueJ20GgnbnkeUTzw6RVnoiMdK4NFovKWr8joZRkLu3vl-zBA2par5RndqqmOdCb7afvu31h-mARQEiiq1p4VDkR-ATgQyWeyedvAkOtrXrXD9EjM_eUPWX7IxCtTo4FMfEG8PG580jqn8RJWwkiFu5GpyYAz2u_MuUQueNy8MNTr18-jCBrAIV9kntOnca3shk3fV41fM5FzfclIe-1pgYZEAL36zNeH-dArADYkJnA_qNglNHYZ6IhkeRvmMEFjcOVMqXz9jI7360LABN569Ug0YcX5wIJo9zSb9OswdIdQ5Tq1PQ1E_1TAw26OcKt_GdsHUaiQoSbsPx9MgwPwTlXRgYE7OhTBThxealXvY1P6yt2soK_4RKXI