ED_ALIBABA_ACCESS_KEY_ID][REDACTED_ALIBABA_ACCESS_KEY_ID][REDACTED_ALIBABA_ACCESS_KEY_ID][REDACTED_ALIBABA_ACCESS_KEY_ID]","detail":"high"},{"type":"input_text","text":"</image>"}]},{"type":"message","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"ai challenge 板块，ai角色血量还是按照正常收到攻击到会倒地；摄像头按照玩家角色为准；ai角色头上亮红色的光点。以ai角色通关为准进入下一个关卡。\n"}]},{"type":"message","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"去掉女性角色，替换为原来的辅助角色\n"}]},{"type":"message","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"检查当前ai角色为何卡顿状态，分析给出结论\n"}]},{"type":"message","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"修复该问题\n"}]},{"type":"message","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"分析目前情况为什么敌人围住ai角色 不攻击\n"}]},{"type":"message","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"继续\n"}]},{"type":"message","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"敌人和玩家和ai角色们都有物理碰撞不能相互穿过\n"}]},{"type":"message","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"每一个章节卡片放两个按钮，一个是challenge 一个是ai challenge；页面地区的ai challenge 去掉\n"}]},{"type":"message","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"ai challenge 游戏里面，玩家的角色也能开宝箱\n"}]},{"type":"message","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"同步代码到git\n"}]},{"type":"message","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"目前是pc版本，该游戏出一个安卓版本的apk；可以在安卓手机安装玩。\n"}]},{"type":"message","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"目前是pc版本，该游戏出一个安卓版本的apk；可以在安卓手机安装玩。需要注意哪些事项，用文字列出\n"}]},{"type":"message","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"列出基础的手机版本需要配置的功能，文字表达\n"}]},{"type":"message","role":"developer","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"<permissions instructions>\nFilesystem sandboxing defines which files can be read or written. `sandbox_mode` is `workspace-write`: The sandbox permits reading files, and editing files in `cwd` and `writable_roots`. Editing files in other directories requires approval. Network access is restricted.\n# Escalation Requests\n\nCommands are run outside the sandbox if they are approved by the user, or match an existing rule that allows it to run unrestricted. The command string is split into independent command segments at shell control operators, including but not limited to:\n\n- Pipes: |\n- Logical operators: &&, ||\n- Command separators: ;\n- Subshell boundaries: (...), $(...)\n\nEach resulting segment is evaluated independently for sandbox restrictions and approval requirements.\n\nExample:\n\ngit pull | tee output.txt\n\nThis is treated as two command segments:\n\n[\"git\", \"pull\"]\n\n[\"tee\", \"output.txt\"]\n\nCommands that use more advanced shell features like redirection (>, >>, <), substitutions ($(...), ...), environment variables (FOO=bar), or wildcard patterns (*, ?) will not be evaluated against rules, to limit the scope of what an approved rule allows.\n\n## How to request escalation\n\nIMPORTANT: To request approval to execute a command that will require escalated privileges:\n\n- Provide the `sandbox_permissions` parameter with the value `\"require_escalated\"`\n- Include a short question asking the user if they want to allow the action in `justification` parameter. e.g. \"Do you want to download and install dependencies for this project?\"\n- Optionally suggest a `prefix_rule` - this will be shown to the user with an option to persist the rule approval for future sessions.\n\nIf you run a command that is important to solving the user's query, but it fails because of sandboxing or with a likely sandbox-related network error (for example DNS/host resolution, registry/index access, or dependency download failure), rerun the command with \"require_escalated\". ALWAYS proceed to use the `justification` parameter - do not message the user before requesting approval for the command.\n\n## When to request escalation\n\nWhile commands are running inside the sandbox, here are some scenarios that will require escalation outside the sandbox:\n\n- You need to run a command that writes to a directory that requires it (e.g. running tests that write to /var)\n- You need to run a GUI app (e.g., open/xdg-open/osascript) to open browsers or files.\n- If you run a command that is important to solving the user's query, but it fails because of sandboxing or with a likely sandbox-related network error (for example DNS/host resolution, registry/index access, or dependency download failure), rerun the command with `require_escalated`. ALWAYS proceed to use the `sandbox_permissions` and `justification` parameters. do not message the user before requesting approval for the command.\n- You are about to take a potentially destructive action such as an `rm` or `git reset` that the user did not explicitly ask for.\n- Be judicious with escalating, but if completing the user's request requires it, you should do so - don't try and circumvent approvals by using other tools.\n\n## prefix_rule guidance\n\nWhen choosing a `prefix_rule`, request one that will allow you to fulfill similar requests from the user in the future without re-requesting escalation. It should be categorical and reasonably scoped to similar capabilities. You should rarely pass the entire command into `prefix_rule`.\n\n### Banned prefix_rules \nAvoid requesting overly broad prefixes that the user would be ill-advised to approve. For example, do not request [\"python3\"], [\"python\", \"-\"], or other similar prefixes that would allow arbitrary scripting.\nNEVER provide a prefix_rule argument for destructive commands like rm.\nNEVER provide a prefix_rule if your command uses a heredoc or herestring. \n\n### Examples\nGood examples of prefixes:\n- [\"npm\", \"run\", \"dev\"]\n- [\"gh\", \"pr\", \"check\"]\n- [\"cargo\", \"test\"]\n\n\n## Approved command prefixes\nThe following prefix rules have already been approved: - [\"gh\"]\n- [\"say\"]\n- [\"kill\"]\n- [\"open\"]\n- [\"/tmp/list-audio-devices\"]\n- [\"/Users/vincet/Library/Android/sdk/emulator/emulator\"]\n- [\"/Users/vincet/Library/Android/sdk/platform-tools/adb\"]\n- [\"/Users/vincet/Documents/aiglass/aigame/tools/set-default-audio-output\"]\n- [\"ps\", \"-Ao\"]\n- [\"ps\", \"-ef\"]\n- [\"curl\", \"-L\"]\n- [\"curl\", \"-s\"]\n- [\"git\", \"add\"]\n- [\"curl\", \"-sL\"]\n- [\"git\", \"push\"]\n- [\"git\", \"clone\"]\n- [\"git\", \"fetch\"]\n- [\"npm\", \"start\"]\n- [\"git\", \"commit\"]\n- [\"xcrun\", \"simctl\"]\n- [\"launchctl\", \"submit\"]\n- [\"killall\", \"coreaudiod\"]\n- [\"node\", \"local-agent-bridge.mjs\"]\n- [\"system_profiler\", \"SPAudioDataType\"]\n- [\"/Users/vincet/Documents/aiglass/aigame/.venv-moss-tts/bin/moss-tts-nano\", \"generate\"]\n- [\"node\", \"/Users/vincet/Documents/aimusic/codex_recovery/restore_ttgame_codex_threads.mjs\"]\n- [\"node\", \"/Users/vincet/Documents/aimusic/codex_recovery/sync_current_codex_to_ttgame_archive.mjs\"]\n- [\"/Users/vincet/.openclaw/tools/node-v22.22.0/bin/node\", \"/Users/vincet/Documents/aiglass/aigame/tools/openclaw-local-bridge.mjs\"]\n- [\"say\", \"-a\", \"92\"]\n- [\"afplay\", \"-a\", \"92\"]\n- [\"rm\", \"-rf\", \"Resources\"]\n- [\"python3\", \"-m\", \"http.server\"]\n- [\"/bin/zsh\", \"-lc\", \"say -a '?' 2>&1\"]\n- [\"pkill\", \"-f\", \"scripts/debot-monitor.mjs\"]\n- [\"sed\", \"-n\", \"/players:/,/ducked players/p\"]\n- [\"curl\", \"-s\", \"http://127.0.0.1:18889/health\"]\n- [\"curl\", \"-s\", \"http://127.0.0.1:18900/health\"]\n- [\"curl\", \"-s\", \"http://127.0.0.1:18901/health\"]\n- [\"curl\", \"-s\", \"http://127.0.0.1:18900/enterprise/minimax\"]\n- [\"launchctl\", \"print\", \"system/com.apple.audio.coreaudiod\"]\n- [\"mkdir\", \"-p\", \"/Users/vincet/Documents/aiglass/aigame/tmp\"]\n- [\"curl\", \"-sL\", \"https://developer.android.com/studio?pkg=studio\"]\n- [\"node\", \"scripts/generate_ios_suno_style_instrumentals.mjs\", \"20\"]\n- [\"/bin/zsh\", \"-lc\", \"ps -ef -ww > /private/tmp/aimuse_ps_chatfix.txt\"]\n- [\"/bin/zsh\", \"-lc\", \"MUSIC_PROXY_HOST=127.0.0.1 node scripts/music_proxy_server.mjs\"]\n- [\"/bin/zsh\", \"-lc\", \"/Users/vincet/Desktop/'Cat Pet.app'/Contents/MacOS/CatPetLauncher\"]\n- [\"curl\", \"-s\", \"http://127.0.0.1:18900/generations/1590d0d2-58a8-4488-aa46-4e514821562c\"]\n- [\"/bin/zsh\", \"-lc\", \"DEBOT_DRY_RUN=1 DEBOT_POLL_MS=600000 node scripts/debot-monitor.mjs\"]\n- [\"/bin/zsh\", \"-lc\", \"find /Users/vincet/Documents -name gradle-wrapper.jar 2>/dev/null | sed -n '1,40p'\"]\n- [\"/bin/zsh\", \"-lc\", \"IOS_MUSIC_BATCH_PREFIX=ios-promptbank-20260507 node scripts/generate_ios_promptbank_via_proxy.mjs 10\"]\n- [\"/bin/zsh\", \"-lc\", \"IOS_MUSIC_BATCH_PREFIX=ios-promptbank-20260507 node scripts/generate_ios_random_instrumentals.mjs 10\"]\n- [\"/bin/zsh\", \"-lc\", \"JAVA_HOME='/Applications/Android Studio.app/Contents/jbr/Contents/Home' ./gradlew assembleDebug --offline\"]\n- [\"/bin/zsh\", \"-lc\", \"/Users/vincet/Library/Android/sdk/platform-tools/adb exec-out screencap -p > /tmp/aimuse_wakelock_fixed.png\"]\n- [\"/bin/zsh\", \"-lc\", \"MUSIC_PROXY_HOST=127.0.0.1 nohup node scripts/music_proxy_server.mjs > /private/tmp/aimuse_music_proxy.log 2>&1 &\"]\n- [\"/bin/zsh\", \"-lc\", \"nohup /Users/vincet/Library/Android/sdk/emulator/emulator -avd FlashPlay_API35 > /private/tmp/ipcard-emulator.log 2>&1 &\"]\n- [\"/bin/zsh\", \"-lc\", \"IOS_MUSIC_BATCH_PREFIX=ios-promptbank-20260507 IOS_PROMPTBANK_TARGET_COUNT=10 node scripts/generate_ios_promptbank_one.mjs 3\"]\n- [\"/bin/zsh\", \"-lc\", \"JAVA_HOME='/Applications/Android Studio.app/Contents/jbr/Contents/Home' ./gradlew :app:installDebug --offline --console=plain\"]\n- [\"/bin/zsh\", \"-lc\", \"/Users/vincet/Library/Android/sdk/platform-tools/adb -s emulator-5554 exec-out screencap -p > /private/tmp/ipcard-reopened.png\"]\n- [\"/bin/zsh\", \"-lc\", \"JAVA_HOME='/Applications/Android Studio.app/Contents/jbr/Contents/Home' ./gradlew :app:assembleDebug --offline --console=plain\"]\n- [\"/bin/zsh\", \"-lc\", \"/Users/vincet/Library/Android/sdk/platform-tools/adb -s emulator-5554 exec-out screencap -p > /private/tmp/ipcard-designers.png\"]\n- [\"/bin/zsh\", \"-lc\", \"/Users/vincet/Library/Android/sdk/platform-tools/adb -s emulator-5554 exec-out screencap -p > /private/tmp/ipcard-douyin-feed.png\"]\n- [\"/bin/zsh\", \"-lc\", \"/Users/vincet/Library/Android/sdk/platform-tools/adb -s emulator-5554 exec-out screencap -p > /private/tmp/card-feed-first-role.png\"]\n- [\"/bin/zsh\", \"-lc\", \"/Users/vincet/Library/Android/sdk/platform-tools/adb -s emulator-5554 exec-out screencap -p > /private/tmp/ipcard-designers-top.png\"]\n- [\"/bin/zsh\", \"-lc\", \"/Users/vincet/Library/Android/sdk/platform-tools/adb -s emulator-5554 exec-out screencap -p > /private/tmp/ipcard-douyin-detail.png\"]\n- [\"/bin/zsh\", \"-lc\", \"/Users/vincet/Library/Android/sdk/platform-tools/adb -s emulator-5554 exec-out screencap -p > /private/tmp/ipcard-imagegen-feed.png\"]\n- [\"/bin/zsh\", \"-lc\", \"/Users/vincet/Library/Android/sdk/platform-tools/adb -s emulator-5554 exec-out screencap -p > /private/tmp/ipcard-purchase-flow.png\"]\n- [\"/bin/zsh\", \"-lc\", \"/Users/vincet/Library/Android/sdk/platform-tools/adb -s emulator-5554 exec-out screencap -p > /private/tmp/ipcard-scope-collected.png\"]\n- [\"/bin/zsh\", \"-lc\", \"/Users/vincet/Lib...\n[Some commands were truncated]\n\n`approvals_reviewer` is `auto_review`: Sandbox escalations with require_escalated will be reviewed for compliance with the policy. If a rejection happens, you should proceed only with a materially safer alternative, or inform the user of the risk and send a final message to ask for approval.\n The writable roots are `/Users/vincet/Documents/aiglass`, `/private/tmp`, `/private/var/folders/nd/fkt5ppxs6635vd7rfflhlc7c0000gn/T`.\n</permissions instructions>"},{"type":"input_text","text":"<app-context>\n# Codex desktop context\n- You are running inside the Codex (desktop) app, which allows some additional features not available in the CLI alone:\n\n### Images/Visuals/Files\n- In the app, the model can display images and videos using standard Markdown image syntax: ![alt](url)\n- When sending or referencing a local image or video, always use an absolute filesystem path in the Markdown image tag (e.g., ![alt](/absolute/path.png)); relative paths and plain text will not render the media.\n- When referencing code or workspace files in responses, always use full absolute file paths instead of relative paths.\n- If a user asks about an image, or asks you to create an image, it is often a good idea to show the image to them in your response.\n- Use mermaid diagrams to represent complex diagrams, graphs, or workflows. Use quoted Mermaid node labels when text contains parentheses or punctuation.\n- Return web URLs as Markdown links (e.g., [label](https://example.com)).\n\n### Workspace Dependencies\n- For sheets, slides, and documents, call `load_workspace_dependencies` to find the bundled runtime and libraries.\n\n### Automations\n- This app supports recurring automations, reminders, monitors, follow-ups, and thread wakeups. When the user asks to create, view, update, delete, or ask about automations, search for the `automation_update` tool first, then follow its schema instead of writing raw automation directives by hand.\n\n### Inline Code Comments\n- Use the ::code-comment{...} directive when you need to attach feedback directly to specific code lines.\n- Emit one directive per inline comment; emit none when there are no actionable inline comments.\n- Required attributes: title (short label), body (one-paragraph explanation), file (path to the file).\n- Optional attributes: start, end (1-based line numbers), priority (0-3).\n- file should be an absolute path or include the workspace folder segment so it can be resolved relative to the workspace.\n- Keep line ranges tight; end defaults to start.\n- Example: ::code-comment{title=\"[P2] Off-by-one\" body=\"Loop iterates past the end when length is 0.\" file=\"/path/to/foo.ts\" start=10 end=11 priority=2}\n\n### Archiving\n- If a user specifically asks you to end a thread/conversation, you can return the archive directive ::archive{...} to archive the thread/conversation.\n- Example: ::archive{reason=\"User requested to end conversation\"}\n</app-context>"},{"type":"input_text","text":"## Memory\n\nYou have access to a memory folder with guidance from prior runs. It can save\ntime and help you stay consistent. Use it whenever it is likely to help.\n\nDecision boundary: should you use memory for a new user query?\n\n- Skip memory ONLY when the request is clearly self-contained and does not need\n  workspace history, conventions, or prior decisions.\n- Hard skip examples: current time/date, simple translation, simple sentence\n  rewrite, one-line shell command, trivial formatting.\n- Use memory by default when ANY of these are true:\n  - the query mentions workspace/repo/module/path/files in MEMORY_SUMMARY below,\n  - the user asks for prior context / consistency / previous decisions,\n  - the task is ambiguous and could depend on earlier project choices,\n  - the ask is a non-trivial and related to MEMORY_SUMMARY below.\n- If unsure, do a quick memory pass.\n\nMemory layout (general -> specific):\n\n- /Users/vincet/.codex/memories/memory_summary.md (already provided below; do NOT open again)\n- /Users/vincet/.codex/memories/MEMORY.md (searchable registry; primary file to query)\n- /Users/vincet/.codex/memories/skills/<skill-name>/ (skill folder)\n  - SKILL.md (entrypoint instructions)\n  - scripts/ (optional helper scripts)\n  - examples/ (optional example outputs)\n  - templates/ (optional templates)\n - /Users/vincet/.codex/memories/rollout_summaries/ (per-rollout recaps + evidence snippets)\n  - The paths of these entries can be found in /Users/vincet/.codex/memories/MEMORY.md or /Users/vincet/.codex/memories/rollout_summaries/ as `rollout_path`\n  - These files are append-only `jsonl`: `session_meta.payload.id` identifies the session, `turn_context` marks turn boundaries, `event_msg` is the lightweight status stream, and `response_item` contains actual messages, tool calls, and tool outputs.\n  - For efficient lookup, prefer matching the filename suffix or `session_meta.payload.id`; avoid broad full-content scans unless needed.\n\nQuick memory pass (when applicable):\n\n1. Skim the MEMORY_SUMMARY below and extract task-relevant keywords.\n2. Search /Users/vincet/.codex/memories/MEMORY.md using those keywords.\n3. Only if MEMORY.md directly points to rollout summaries/skills, open the 1-2\n   most relevant files under /Users/vincet/.codex/memories/rollout_summaries/ or\n   /Users/vincet/.codex/memories/skills/.\n4. If above are not clear and you need exact commands, error text, or precise evidence, search over `rollout_path` for more evidence.\n5. If there are no relevant hits, stop memory lookup and continue normally.\n\nQuick-pass budget:\n\n- Keep memory lookup lightweight: ideally <= 4-6 search steps before main work.\n- Avoid broad scans of all rollout summaries.\n\nDuring execution: if you hit repeated errors, confusing behavior, or suspect\nrelevant prior context, redo the quick memory pass.\n\nHow to decide whether to verify memory:\n\n- Consider both risk of drift and verification effort.\n- If a fact is likely to drift and is cheap to verify, verify it before\n  answering.\n- If a fact is likely to drift but verification is expensive, slow, or\n  disruptive, it is acceptable to answer from memory in an interactive turn,\n  but you should say that it is memory-derived, note that it may be stale, and\n  consider offering to refresh it live.\n- If a fact is lower-drift and cheap to verify, use judgment: verification is\n  more important when the fact is central to the answer or especially easy to\n  confirm.\n- If a fact is lower-drift and expensive to verify, it is usually fine to\n  answer from memory directly.\n\nWhen answering from memory without current verification:\n\n- If you rely on memory for a fact that you did not verify in the current turn,\n  say so briefly in the final answer.\n- If that fact is plausibly drift-prone or comes from an older note, older\n  snapshot, or prior run summary, say that it may be stale or outdated.\n- If live verification was skipped and a refresh would be useful in the\n  interactive context, consider offering to verify or refresh it live.\n- Do not present unverified memory-derived facts as confirmed-current.\n- For interactive requests, prefer a short refresh offer over silently doing\n  expensive verification that the user did not ask for.\n- When the unverified fact is about prior results, commands, timing, or an\n  older snapshot, a concrete refresh offer can be especially helpful.\n\nMemory citation requirements:\n\n- If ANY relevant memory files were used: append exactly one\n`<oai-mem-citation>` block as the VERY LAST content of the final reply.\n  Normal responses should include the answer first, then append the\n`<oai-mem-citation>` block at the end.\n- Use this exact structure for programmatic parsing:\n```\n<oai-mem-citation>\n<citation_entries>\nMEMORY.md:234-236|note=[responsesapi citation extraction code pointer]\nrollout_summaries/2026-02-17T21-23-02-LN3m-weekly_memory_report_pivot_from_git_history.md:10-12|note=[weekly report format]\n</citation_entries>\n<rollout_ids>\n019c6e27-e55b-73d1-87d8-4e01f1f75043\n019c7714-3b77-74d1-9866-e1f484aae2ab\n</rollout_ids>\n</oai-mem-citation>\n```\n- `citation_entries` is for rendering:\n  - one citation entry per line\n  - format: `<file>:<line_start>-<line_end>|note=[<how memory was used>]`\n  - use file paths relative to the memory base path (for example, `MEMORY.md`,\n    `rollout_summaries/...`, `skills/...`)\n  - only cite files actually used under the memory base path (do not cite\n    workspace files as memory citations)\n  - if you used `MEMORY.md` and then a rollout summary/skill file, cite both\n  - list entries in order of importance (most important first)\n  - `note` should be short, single-line, and use simple characters only (avoid\n    unusual symbols, no newlines)\n- `rollout_ids` is for us to track what previous rollouts you find useful:\n  - include one rollout id per line\n  - rollout ids should look like UUIDs (for example,\n    `019c6e27-e55b-73d1-87d8-4e01f1f75043`)\n  - include unique ids only; do not repeat ids\n  - an empty `<rollout_ids>` section is allowed if no rollout ids are available\n  - you can find rollout ids in rollout summary files and MEMORY.md\n  - do not include file paths or notes in this section\n  - For every `citation_entries`, try to find and cite the corresponding rollout id if possible\n- Never include memory citations inside pull-request messages.\n- Never cite blank lines; double-check ranges.\n\nUpdating memories:\n\nYou can update the memories **only** when explicitly asked by the user. This must always come from a direct request from the user.\n- Write your update in /Users/vincet/.codex/memories/extensions/ad_hoc/notes/\n- Each update must be one small file containing what you want to add/delete/update from the memories.\n- The name of this file must be `<timestamp>-<short slug>.md`\n- Do not try to edit the memory files yourself, only add one update note in /Users/vincet/.codex/memories/extensions/ad_hoc/notes/\n\n========= MEMORY_SUMMARY BEGINS =========\n## User Profile\n\nThe user runs several local prototypes in parallel and treats Codex, Godot, iPhone Simulator, Chrome, Finder, Telegram, and desktop app windows as one working surface, often in Chinese. Current high-activity scopes include `/Users/vincet/Documents/aiglass/godot-2d-treasure`, `/Users/vincet/Documents/aiglass/aigame`, `/Users/vincet/Documents/New project/KidCreatorMac`, BSC/DeBot monitoring, and `/Users/vincet/Desktop/aigame2`. [chronicle memory]\n\nThey usually do not accept code-only claims. Good collaboration means verifying through the live surface that matters for the task: Godot debug windows for gameplay and AI behavior, iPhone Simulator for iOS state, Chrome for dashboards or provider pages, Telegram for delivery, and Finder for exact asset provenance. [chronicle memory]\n\nThey preserve exact routing handles and expect future agents to do the same: project paths, filenames, bundle ids, config keys, contract strings, commands, model names, error text, and visible UI copy. They also work through unstable proxy/network and local-backend conditions, so `自由猫`, `ERR_NETWORK_CHANGED`, `127.0.0.1:18900`, and similar environment details are often part of the task context rather than background noise. [chronicle memory]\n\n## User preferences\n\n- In Godot work, move quickly from plan to a runnable debug window; do not stop at ideation if the next step can be a playable prototype. [chronicle memory]\n- For `/Users/vincet/Documents/aiglass/godot-2d-treasure`, keep early versions small when the request is “a dungeon treasure-hunting game” and “not add dungeon enemies,” then grow systems only after the loop is playable. [chronicle memory]\n- Once a playable Godot loop exists, separate gameplay changes from visual-skin changes; if the request is “pure exploration, coin/gem collection” plus a palette/art swap, do not redesign the loop. [chronicle memory]\n- In `godot-2d-treasure` tuning passes, expect concrete live corrections like “the slime still stuck to the character,” “the hit animation is wrong,” persistent Chapter 3 bubbles, teammate spacing, or attack-lock behavior; keep the game running and fix the exact interaction. [chronicle memory]\n- After combat/input changes in `godot-2d-treasure`, keep manual playtesting until live outcomes such as `Attack missed.`, `Arrow shot missed.`, `Frozen`, `You collapsed.`, `Healing fountain restored +1 HP`, or visible bubble/fireball behavior appear. [chronicle memory]\n- When Chapter 2/3/4 work is in play, preserve chapter-specific logic and art instead of flattening everything into one biome or one combat rule set. [chronicle memory]\n- When chapter-menu polish is requested in `Quiet Dungeon Treasure`, reuse existing chapter assets, keep the work menu-only, and visually inspect the live `Choose Chapter` screen after each pass. [chronicle memory]\n- When AI Challenge is being debugged in `godot-2d-treasure`, prefer real-time state/strategy iteration over fixed autoplay. Make takeover/fallback state explicit and keep the JSON/state/log files visible. [chronicle memory]\n- When AI Challenge needs to be restored after test-heavy supervisor work, bring back normal chapter/level progression first. Keep the main AI character as `player`, add `assist_player` only as a helper, and keep camera/exit/AI state anchored to `player`. [chronicle memory]\n- When the AI Challenge helper is tied to a generated sprite sheet, preserve the chosen asset/action library and the narrower move set: normal attack plus a `moon-slash`-style second attack, without reviving old bow/ice behavior by accident. [chronicle memory]\n- In FlashPlay/`aigame`, “backend fixed” still means the booted simulator shows the expected app state or generated game (`Cat Catch`, `Doggy Ball Chase`, `Me`) after relaunch. [chronicle memory]\n- For FlashPlay/GaAiGame debugging, isolate the failing layer precisely: `Could not connect to the server`, backend port `18900` down, `minimax_api_error:2049:invalid api key`, `minimax_chat_failed:401`, `providerOrder: deepseek`, and `deepseek.configured: true` are different stages, not one generic backend problem. [chronicle memory]\n- When backend or env material is on screen, preserve the file path and config-key names (`backend/.env`, `DEEPSEEK_API_KEY`, `INFOPLIST_KEY_FLASHPLAY_BACKEND_BASE_URL`) but never retain secret values. [chronicle memory]\n- When deployment readiness is being discussed, do not promote generated Cloud Run guidance into “done” unless there is separate execution evidence. [chronicle memory]\n- In mobile-app verification threads like FlashPlay or Aimuse, answer from the current simulator screen first; if something is “missing” or “failed,” check the visible account/app state before assuming the feature never shipped. [chronicle memory]\n- For FlashPlay account deletion/reset, verify the visible `Generated` and `Favorites` lists after the action and explicitly separate account-scoped cleanup from settings that intentionally remain local, such as language or device id. [chronicle memory]\n- When building the Codex-like app, keep it capability-based and child-friendly: `问一问`, `做 PPT`, `做网页`, `学代码`, parent-managed keys, local save, and execution confirmation are part of the intended surface. [chronicle memory]\n- In `KidCreatorMac`, use a separate app skeleton instead of disturbing adjacent experiments; the visible direction was explicitly not to touch existing OneNode/agentwallet files. [chronicle memory]\n- For `KidCreatorMac`, prefer real outputs and live validation over mockup-only progress: save/export artifacts, reopen the built app, and inspect the actual window. [chronicle memory]\n- If the user asks for a Codex-like composer in `KidCreatorMac`, focus layout/style changes on the floating input feel instead of mixing that work with provider/export rewrites. [chronicle memory]\n- When a monitor/backend is reported ready, do not stop at “backend complete”; validate the delivery surface, dashboard state, and process state together. [chronicle memory]\n- For BSC/DeBot monitoring, preserve exact contract/token handles and cross-check across DeBot, X, and holder-analysis pages instead of answering from one tab. [chronicle memory]\n- When a crypto dashboard or social feed flips into `ERR_NETWORK_CHANGED`, `暂无数据`, or `Something went wrong. Try reloading.`, check proxy state such as `自由猫` mode/node selection before treating it as a clean application bug. [chronicle memory]\n- For token-monitoring delivery, make the first section easy to copy: `project name + CA` first, then add extra prose only if it survives the channel cleanly. [chronicle memory]\n- If no bot/API sending path is confirmed yet, use visible desktop Telegram first instead of blocking the workflow on bot setup. [chronicle memory]\n- In the `aigame2` music workflow, continue one instrumental/score track at a time and check whether the interrupted item is still running or already landed in `manifest.json` before scheduling the next pass. [chronicle memory]\n- If a music item fails with `fetch failed`, record the exact title and move to the next index after checking for leftover process state instead of replaying the same broken item. [chronicle memory]\n- When the user pivots into local-skill lookup, keep exact installed skill names and source paths (`measure-okr-grader`, `utility-update-pm-skills`, `Presentations`, `foundation-meeting-brief`) instead of abstract “presentation tooling” language. [chronicle memory]\n- In browser image-to-3D work, reuse the exact local PNG chain across Tripo, Meshy, Hi3D, Neural4D, and adjacent tools before inventing a new prompt or reference set. [chronicle memory]\n- When evaluating image-to-3D tools, inspect real model metadata first: geometry stats, `bind skeleton` / `biped`, export settings, license/privacy state, and credit cost. [chronicle memory]\n- When a browser tool hits `server busy`, login friction, credit refund, or content-policy refusal, keep the same PNG chain and move to the next service rather than restarting ideation. [chronicle memory]\n- For wallet-confirmed NFT work, keep the trust boundary explicit: the assistant can analyze, prepare, and explain, but the wallet still confirms the final transaction. [chronicle memory]\n- In `KidCreatorMac` packaging work, treat “works on a new computer” as part of done: verify the bundled runtime from `/private/tmp/kidcreator-no-codex-home`, not just the current machine. [chronicle memory]\n\n## General Tips\n\n- Use the visible live surface as the truth source: Godot, Simulator, Chrome, Telegram, Finder, and Blender state outrank an unverified code-only claim. [chronicle memory]\n- Preserve exact filenames, project paths, scene names, bundle ids, contract strings, ports, model names, commands, and error text. The user’s workflows depend on those handles later. [chronicle memory]\n- For `/Users/vincet/Documents/aiglass/godot-2d-treasure`, check `README.md`, `assets/ASSET_PROVENANCE.md`, `project.godot`, `scenes/main.tscn`, and especially `scripts/main.gd` first. [chronicle memory]\n- In later `godot-2d-treasure` work, remember that generated preview maps and actual playable layouts drifted apart once; verify shared generation/layout logic before trusting contact sheets alone. [chronicle memory]\n- For `Quiet Dungeon Treasure` AI Challenge work, the first files and paths to grep are `/private/tmp/quiet_dungeon_treasure_ai_state.json`, `/private/tmp/quiet_dungeon_treasure_ai_command.json`, `/private/tmp/quiet_dungeon_treasure_ai_challenge_log.json`, `/private/tmp/quiet_dungeon_treasure_ai_strategy_log.json`, and `tools/codex_ai_supervisor.mjs`. [chronicle memory]\n- For the later `Quiet Dungeon Treasure` helper-character pass, add `/private/tmp/quiet_dungeon_start_ai_challenge.gd`, `codex_ai_apply_objective_assist()`, `tools/generate_moon_swordswoman_asset.py`, `assets/generated/moon_swordswoman.png`, `assets/generated/character_action_library.json`, and `assets…843 tokens truncated…k whether control is truly external, whether progress fields change while the run is live, and whether no-progress, `objective_position_correction`, backtracking through a “return to previous layer” door, or attack-lock symptoms are coming from stale timers, too-small exit-rush thresholds, missed animation callbacks, or bad supervisor targets.\n\n- Quiet Dungeon Treasure chapter-menu polish and four-chapter live QA: `Choose Chapter`, `Chapter 1 - Slime`, `Chapter 3 - Tide Gate Reef`, `Chapter 4 - Ash Gate`, `80 pixels`, `Healing fountain restored +6 HP`, `Chapter Complete`, `You Collapsed`\n  - desc: Use this topic for late May 16 chapter-wide playtesting and presentation work: teammate spacing, Chapter 2/3/4 combat validation, healing-fountain checks, collapse/retry loops, and the redesign from static chapter cards to themed animated previews.\n  - learnings: menu work was intentionally menu-only and reused in-game assets. The live proof points were chapter-card readability, non-wandering enemy loops, follower spacing that stopped overlap, and real runs through Forest, Ocean, and Volcano instead of code-only validation.\n\n- Quiet Dungeon Treasure chapter projectile cleanup and earlier chapter expansion: `MENU`, `reef monsters now make ranged bubble attacks`, `bubble speed 115`, `1.8s`, `Chapter 2: Forest`, `undersea ruins`, `Chapter 4 - Ash Gate`, `QuietDungeonTreasure.app`\n  - desc: Use this topic for the preceding chapter-buildout arc in the same checkout: chapter select, forest-specific rules, undersea planning, volcano assets, launcher packaging, and the first Chapter 3/4 projectile cleanup pass.\n  - learnings: the stable pattern is chapter-specific assets and logic plus live validation through the debug window or launcher, not generic biome swaps.\n\n### /Users/vincet/Documents/aiglass/aigame\n\n#### 2026-05-16\n\n- FlashPlay DeepSeek provider wiring, simulator relaunch, and Cloud Run MVP readiness: `com.flashplay.demo`, `Cat Catch`, `Doggy Ball Chase`, `providerOrder: deepseek`, `deepseek.configured: true`, `backend/.env`, `INFOPLIST_KEY_FLASHPLAY_BACKEND_BASE_URL`, `TaskStore`, `flashplay-backend`\n  - desc: Search here first for exact `cwd=/Users/vincet/Documents/aiglass/aigame` work on local DeepSeek-backed generation, simulator-visible validation, and the later deployment-readiness checklist.\n  - learnings: the freshest split is local backend proof vs generated Cloud Run guidance. Confirm simulator state after relaunch, and treat Cloud Run steps as checklist-only until command and endpoint execution are shown.\n\n- FlashPlay account deletion and guest-reset verification: `Account deletion`, `Continue as Guest`, `Generated`, `Favorites`, `2048`, `24Points`, `Cat Jump`, `language selection`, `device id`\n  - desc: Use this topic for the `Me`-tab deletion/reset workflow: where the control appears, what backend/local data are supposed to clear, and how the generated/favorites lists should look after guest reset or Firestore-backed deletion.\n  - learnings: verify the actual post-delete lists in Simulator. The useful distinction is account-scoped cards vs intentionally retained local settings, not just whether the API route exists.\n\n### workflow:flashplay-gaaigame-mobile-generation-debug\n\n#### 2026-05-15\n\n- FlashPlay/GaAiGame `Cat Catch` MiniMax failure isolation: `Could not connect to the server`, `minimax:minimax_api_error:2049:invalid api key`, `minimax:minimax_chat_failed:401`, `127.0.0.1:18900`, `backend/.env`\n  - desc: Search here first for the iPhone Simulator tiny-game generator debugging loop that preceded the May 16 DeepSeek pass: online-backend vs local-backend routing, MiniMax key/endpoint diagnosis, and exact simulator repro steps from the `Cat Catch` create screen.\n  - learnings: the main decision trigger is the error transition. If the failure changes from connection to provider-key to provider-auth, the routing changed and the remaining bug is more specific than “backend broken.”\n\n### /Users/vincet/Documents/New project/KidCreatorMac\n\n#### 2026-05-15\n\n- KidCreatorMac scaffold, export surface, provider debugging, and packaging proof: `PM skill;`, `DeepSeekClient.swift`, `ArtifactExporter.swift`, `MiniMax-M2.7`, `presentation.pptx`, `Kid-Creator-0.1.0.dmg`, `/private/tmp/kidcreator-no-codex-home`\n  - desc: Use this topic for the May 15 SwiftUI/macOS child-friendly Codex-like assistant app: product architecture, independent app scaffold, artifact export, floating-composer UX, provider/keychain work, and later PPT/DMG packaging verification.\n  - learnings: preserve the independent-app boundary, validate through the live `Kid Creator` window, and treat “works on a new computer” as part of done.\n\n### workflow:debot-bsc-signal-monitor-and-token-cross-check\n\n#### 2026-05-14\n\n- BSC DeBot monitor, resident script, and contract cross-check: `chain=bsc&tab=signal`, `run-debot-bsc-desktop-monitor.sh`, `debot-bsc-monitor.mjs`, `scanned=0`, `ERR_NETWORK_CHANGED`, `自由猫`\n  - desc: Use this topic for the BSC-specific monitoring workflow: live DeBot dashboard watching, X/holder-analysis cross-checking, resident desktop monitor setup, and proxy-aware troubleshooting.\n  - learnings: preserve both the process handle and the contract handle. `scanned=0` and proxy state belong in the same diagnostic picture as dashboard failures.\n\n### /Users/vincet/Desktop/aigame2\n\n#### 2026-05-14\n\n- `aigame2` one-track continuation and Aimuse simulator recovery: `Night Train Downtempo`, `fetch failed`, `manifest unchanged`, `86 tracks`, `item 32 failed`, `continue item 33`, `Aimus.app/seed_music`, `com.museplay.ios`\n  - desc: Search here for the latest `cwd=/Users/vincet/Desktop/aigame2` recovery state: manifest-aware one-track continuation after `fetch failed`, plus the adjacent Aimuse simulator relaunch/check that confirmed the music UI and generated-song list actually came back.\n  - learnings: if a batch item fails, keep the exact title, verify no leftover process or manifest write, then move forward. When restart/build status is uncertain, confirm the live simulator list/detail view and counters instead of trusting tool output alone.\n\n### Older Memory Topics\n\n#### workflow:ai-skill-stack-research\n\n- Local PM/OKR and presentation skill lookup in Codex: `measure-okr-grader`, `utility-update-pm-skills`, `Presentations`, `utility-slideshow-creator`, `/Users/vincet/Documents/skills/DESIGN.md`\n  - desc: Use this topic for exact installed PM/presentation skill names and source paths; search here before answering any local-skill lookup request.\n\n#### /Users/vincet/Documents/aiglass/godot-codex-lab\n\n- Godot Codex Lab ideation and five-map progression: `3D multi-map teleport/exploration game`, `5 maps`, `HUD x / 5`, `final-door unlock`, `Attempting to use a shader that requires tangents`\n  - desc: Covers the earlier 3D Godot lab thread: tangent-warning/import context plus the later concrete five-map progression/gating plan in this checkout.\n\n#### workflow:flashplay-ios-account-deletion-verification\n\n- FlashPlay account deletion simulator verification: `FlashPlay`, `cd0423a Add account deletion flow`, `f75bdca Add Firestore-backed account deletion`, `Me`, `Account deletion`, `Continue as Guest`\n  - desc: Covers `Me`-tab verification of guest-vs-signed-in state, backend/local cleanup semantics, and the later May 16 generated/favorites reset checks in Simulator.\n\n#### workflow:debot-solana-signal-monitor-validation\n\n- DeBot monitor dry run, Telegram validation, and proxy troubleshooting: `.env.debot-monitor`, `scripts/run-debot-monitor.sh`, `DEBOT_DRY_RUN=1`, `Vinto sats`, `暂无数据`, `自由猫`\n  - desc: Covers the Solana/ETH monitor-verification workflow: Telegram delivery, dry-run command/process checks, dashboard watching, and proxy-aware troubleshooting.\n\n#### workflow:sol-eth-hot-project-monitoring-and-telegram-delivery\n\n- SOL/ETH hot-project monitoring to Telegram delivery: `eth-sol-p0-new-projects-30m-watch`, `Vinto sats`, `上线 30 分钟内优质候选`, `project name + CA`, `RKC/SOL`, `WORLDCUP/SOL`\n  - desc: Covers the Chinese monitoring prompts, the every-30-minutes watch update, BotFather setup around `@meme1188_bot`, Telegram delivery, and CA-first formatting pivots.\n\n#### workflow:visual-asset-generation-and-web3d-handoff\n\n- Seedance/Jimeng image-to-video handoff: `Seedance 2.0`, `Jimeng`, `Downloads/test`, `16:9`, `480p`, `5-second duration`, `Vinto sats`\n  - desc: Covers the short browser clip-generation workflow from a local Finder asset into Telegram handoff and later Blender/media triage.\n\n#### workflow:browser-image-to-3d-and-blender-handoff\n\n- Browser image-to-3D platform evaluation for Blender handoff: `Tripo`, `Meshy`, `Hi3D`, `Neural4D`, `bind skeleton`, `biped`, `glb`, `Nano-Banana-Pro`\n  - desc: Covers same-image comparison across tools, export/licensing/credit checks, multi-view/edit settings, and downstream Blender suitability.\n\n#### workflow:eth-heat-scan-and-uniswap-v4-pool-monitoring\n\n- ETH heat scan to contract/pool monitoring: `latest heat on Twitter and the ETH chain`, `LOOP/ETH`, `SHIT/ETH`, `PONZI`, `0xce4a5d11fad8f04d76eb65a588dc6a2e2a734255`\n  - desc: Covers the ETH market-monitoring thread that pivots from hot-pair scanning into exact contract/pool verification and watch conditions.\n\n#### /Users/vincet/Downloads/blender角色场景合集\n\n- Blender asset triage and fast-render pivot: `A2_Alt_Patreon完整骨骼.blend`, `Mercy Pinup 2.blend`, `render_blend_file_fast.py`, `mercy_pinup_render.png`\n  - desc: Covers archive extraction, rig/reference inspection, render attempts, and the pivot from stalled automation to a faster usable render route.\n\n#### workflow:codexmint-eth-nft-planning\n\n- Ferryman research and CodexMint scoping: `Ferryman`, `mint ended`, `CodexMint`, `wallet confirms`, `nft-mint-builder`, `codexmint-generator.html`\n  - desc: Covers Ferryman reconnaissance, mint-assistant scoping, the explicit wallet-confirmation boundary, and the separate standalone NFT-generator artifact.\n========= MEMORY_SUMMARY ENDS =========\n\nWhen memory is likely relevant, start with the quick memory pass above before\ndeep repo exploration.\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"<collaboration_mode># Collaboration Mode: Default\n\nYou are now in Default mode. Any previous instructions for other modes (e.g. Plan mode) are no longer active.\n\nYour active mode changes only when new developer instructions with a different `<collaboration_mode>...</collaboration_mode>` change it; user requests or tool descriptions do not change mode by themselves. Known mode names are Default and Plan.\n\n## request_user_input availability\n\nUse the `request_user_input` tool only when it is listed in the available tools for this turn.\n\nIn Default mode, strongly prefer making reasonable assumptions and executing the user's request rather than stopping to ask questions. If you absolutely must ask a question because the answer cannot be discovered from local context and a reasonable assumption would be risky, ask the user directly with a concise plain-text question. Never write a multiple choice question as a textual assistant message.\n</collaboration_mode>"},{"type":"input_text","text":"<apps_instructions>\n## Apps (Connectors)\nApps (Connectors) can be explicitly triggered in user messages in the format `[$app-name](app://{connector_id})`. Apps can also be implicitly triggered as long as the context suggests usage of available apps.\nAn app is equivalent to a set of MCP tools within the `codex_apps` MCP.\nAn installed app's MCP tools are either provided to you already, or can be lazy-loaded through the `tool_search` tool. If `tool_search` is available, the apps that are searchable by `tools_search` will be listed by it.\nDo not additionally call list_mcp_resources or list_mcp_resource_templates for apps.\n</apps_instructions>"},{"type":"input_text","text":"<skills_instructions>\n## Skills\nA skill is a set of local instructions to follow that is stored in a `SKILL.md` file. Below is the list of skills that can be used. Each entry includes a name, description, and a short path that can be expanded into an absolute path using the skill roots table.\n### Skill roots\n- `r0` = `/Users/vincet/.codex/skills`\n- `r1` = `/Users/vincet/.codex/skills/.system`\n- `r2` = `/Users/vincet/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-bundled`\n- `r3` = `/Users/vincet/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated/build-ios-apps/63976030/skills`\n- `r4` = `/Users/vincet/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated/build-macos-apps/63976030/skills`\n- `r5` = `/Users/vincet/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated/build-web-apps/63976030/skills`\n- `r6` = `/Users/vincet/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated/circleci/63976030/skills`\n- `r7` = `/Users/vincet/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated/cloudflare/63976030/skills`\n- `r8` = `/Users/vincet/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated`\n- `r9` = `/Users/vincet/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated/game-studio/63976030/skills`\n- `r10` = `/Users/vincet/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated/github/63976030/skills`\n- `r11` = `/Users/vincet/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated/stripe/63976030/skills`\n- `r12` = `/Users/vincet/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated/test-android-apps/63976030/skills`\n- `r13` = `/Users/vincet/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-primary-runtime`\n### Available skills\n- imagegen: Generate or edit raster images when the task benefits from AI-created bitmap visuals such as photos, illustrations, textures, sprites, mockups, or transparent-background cutouts. Use when Codex should create a brand-new image, transform an existi (file: r1/imagegen/SKILL.md)\n- openai-docs: Use when the user asks how to build with OpenAI products or APIs and needs up-to-date official documentation with citations, help choosing the latest model for a use case, or model upgrade and prompt-upgrade guidance; prioritize OpenAI docs MCP (file: r1/openai-docs/SKILL.md)\n- plugin-creator: Create and scaffold plugin directories for Codex with a required `.codex-plugin/plugin.json`, optional plugin folders/files, and baseline placeholders you can edit before publishing or testing. Use when Codex needs to create a new local plugin, a (file: r1/plugin-creator/SKILL.md)\n- skill-creator: Guide for creating effective skills. This skill should be used when users want to create a new skill (or update an existing skill) that extends Codex's capabilities with specialized knowledge, workflows, or tool integrations. (file: r1/skill-creator/SKILL.md)\n- skill-installer: Install Codex skills into $CODEX_HOME/skills from a curated list or a GitHub repo path. Use when a user asks to list installable skills, install a curated skill, or install a skill from another repo (including private repos). (file: r1/skill-installer/SKILL.md)\n- browser-use:browser: Browser automation for the Codex in-app browser. Use to open, navigate, inspect, test, click, type, screenshot, or verify local targets such as localhost, 127.0.0.1, ::1, file://, the current in-app browser tab, and websites shown side by side  (file: r2/browser-use/0.1.0-alpha2/skills/browser/SKILL.md)\n- build-ios-apps:ios-app-intents: Design and implement App Intents, app entities, and App Shortcuts for iOS apps so useful actions and content are available to Shortcuts, Siri, Spotlight, widgets, controls, and other intent-driven system surfaces. Use when exposing app actions o (file: r3/ios-app-intents/SKILL.md)\n- build-ios-apps:ios-debugger-agent: Use XcodeBuildMCP to build, run, launch, and debug the current iOS project on a booted simulator. Trigger when asked to run an iOS app, interact with the simulator UI, inspect on-screen state, capture logs/console output, or diagnose runtime behav (file: r3/ios-debugger-agent/SKILL.md)\n- build-ios-apps:ios-ettrace-performance: Capture and interpret ETTrace profiles for iOS simulator apps, including symbolicated launch and runtime flamegraphs. Use when asked to profile an iOS app flow, gather simulator performance traces, identify CPU-heavy stacks, compare before/after (file: r3/ios-ettrace-performance/SKILL.md)\n- build-ios-apps:ios-memgraph-leaks: Capture, inspect, compare, and root-cause iOS memory graph leaks using Apple's leaks and memgraph tools. Use when debugging leaked iOS objects, simulator memgraphs, retain-cycle suspicions, memory growth after navigation/logout/account changes, or (file: r3/ios-memgraph-leaks/SKILL.md)\n- build-ios-apps:swiftui-liquid-glass: Implement, review, or improve SwiftUI features using the iOS 26+ Liquid Glass API. Use when asked to adopt Liquid Glass in new SwiftUI UI, refactor an existing feature to Liquid Glass, or review Liquid Glass usage for correctness, performance, and (file: r3/swiftui-liquid-glass/SKILL.md)\n- build-ios-apps:swiftui-performance-audit: Audit and improve SwiftUI runtime performance from code review and architecture. Use for requests to diagnose slow rendering, janky scrolling, high CPU/memory usage, excessive view updates, or layout thrash in SwiftUI apps, and to provide guidan (file: r3/swiftui-performance-audit/SKILL.md)\n- build-ios-apps:swiftui-ui-patterns: Best practices and example-driven guidance for building SwiftUI views and components, including navigation hierarchies, custom view modifiers, and responsive layouts with stacks and grids. Use when creating or refactoring SwiftUI UI, designing t (file: r3/swiftui-ui-patterns/SKILL.md)\n- build-ios-apps:swiftui-view-refactor: Refactor and review SwiftUI view files with strong defaults for small dedicated subviews, MV-over-MVVM data flow, stable view trees, explicit dependency injection, and correct Observation usage. Use when cleaning up a SwiftUI view, splitting lon (file: r3/swiftui-view-refactor/SKILL.md)\n- build-macos-apps:appkit-interop: Decide when and how to bridge a macOS app from SwiftUI into AppKit. Use when implementing NSViewRepresentable or NSViewControllerRepresentable, accessing NSWindow or the responder chain, presenting panels, customizing menus, or handling desktop  (file: r4/appkit-interop/SKILL.md)\n- build-macos-apps:build-run-debug: Build, run, and debug local macOS apps and desktop executables using shell-first Xcode and Swift workflows. Use when asked to build a Mac app, launch it, diagnose compiler or linker failures, inspect startup problems, or debug desktop-only runtime (file: r4/build-run-debug/SKILL.md)\n- build-macos-apps:liquid-glass: Implement, refactor, or review modern macOS SwiftUI UI for the new design system and Liquid Glass. Use when adopting Liquid Glass, updating NavigationSplitView, toolbars, search, sheets, and controls, removing custom backgrounds that fight syste (file: r4/liquid-glass/SKILL.md)\n- build-macos-apps:packaging-notarization: Prepare and troubleshoot packaging, signing, and notarization workflows for macOS distribution. Use when asked to archive a Mac app, validate bundle structure, reason about notarization readiness, or explain distribution-only failures. (file: r4/packaging-notarization/SKILL.md)\n- build-macos-apps:signing-entitlements: Inspect signing, entitlements, hardened runtime, and Gatekeeper issues for macOS apps. Use when asked to diagnose code signing failures, missing entitlements, sandbox problems, notarization prerequisites, or trust-policy launch errors. (file: r4/signing-entitlements/SKILL.md)\n- build-macos-apps:swiftpm-macos: Build, run, and test pure SwiftPM-based macOS packages and executables. Use when the repo is package-first, when there is no Xcode project, or when Swift package workflows are the fastest path to diagnosis. (file: r4/swiftpm-macos/SKILL.md)\n- build-macos-apps:swiftui-patterns: Best practices and example-driven guidance for building native macOS SwiftUI scenes and components, including windows, commands, toolbars, settings, split views, inspectors, menu bar extras, and keyboard-driven workflows. Use when creating or re (file: r4/swiftui-patterns/SKILL.md)\n- build-macos-apps:telemetry: Add lightweight runtime telemetry and debug instrumentation to macOS apps, then verify those events after building and running. Use when wiring `Logger` / `os.Logger`, adding log points for window/sidebar/menu-bar actions, reading runtime logs fro (file: r4/telemetry/SKILL.md)\n- build-macos-apps:test-triage: Triage failing macOS tests across Xcode and SwiftPM workflows. Use when asked to run macOS tests, narrow failing scopes, explain assertion or crash failures, or separate real test regressions from setup and environment problems. (file: r4/test-triage/SKILL.md)\n- build-macos-apps:view-refactor: Refactor macOS SwiftUI views and scenes with strong defaults for small dedicated subviews, stable sidebar and selection structure, explicit command and toolbar ownership, scene-aware state, and narrow AppKit escape hatches. Use when cleaning up a  (file: r4/view-refactor/SKILL.md)\n- build-macos-apps:window-management: Customize macOS 15+ SwiftUI windows and scene behavior using Window, WindowGroup, and macOS window modifiers. Use when styling or hiding window toolbars and titles, extending drag regions with WindowDragGesture, replacing window backgrounds with m (file: r4/window-management/SKILL.md)\n- build-web-apps:frontend-app-builder: Use for new frontend applications, dashboards, games, creative websites, hero sections, and visually driven UI from scratch, or when the user explicitly asks for a redesign/restyle/modernization. Builds from clean, airy, high-taste, readable image (file: r5/frontend-app-builder/SKILL.md)\n- build-web-apps:frontend-testing-debugging: Use when testing, debugging, or making targeted improvements to rendered frontend apps through the Build Web Apps or web dev plugin: local dev servers, UI regressions, interaction bugs, console errors, responsive layout, and visual QA. Check wheth (file: r5/frontend-testing-debugging/SKILL.md)\n- build-web-apps:react-best-practices: React and Next.js performance optimization guidelines from Vercel Engineering. This skill should be used when writing, reviewing, or refactoring React/Next.js code to ensure optimal performance patterns. Triggers on tasks involving React compo (file: r5/react-best-practices/SKILL.md)\n- build-web-apps:shadcn: Manages shadcn components and projects — adding, searching, fixing, debugging, styling, and composing UI. Provides project context, component docs, and usage examples. Applies when working with shadcn/ui, component registries, presets, --preset c (file: r5/shadcn-best-practices/SKILL.md)\n- build-web-apps:stripe-best-practices: Guides Stripe integration decisions — API selection (Checkout Sessions vs PaymentIntents), Connect platform setup (Accounts v2, controller properties), billing/subscriptions, Treasury financial accounts, integration surfaces (Checkout, Payment (file: r5/stripe-best-practices/SKILL.md)\n- build-web-apps:supabase-postgres-best-practices: Postgres performance optimization and best practices from Supabase. Use this skill when writing, reviewing, or optimizing Postgres queries, schema designs, or database configurations. (file: r5/supabase-best-practices/SKILL.md)\n- chronicle: Allows you to view the user's screen as well as several hours of history. Use when the user makes a reference to their recent work, for which it'd be helpful to see the screen. This skill MUST be used whenever you need to resolve ambiguity in a (file: r0/chronicle/SKILL.md)\n- circleci:chunk: Use CircleCI Chunk for AI-assisted CI/CD work through either the Chunk web UI or the chunk-cli. Trigger this skill when users ask to set up Chunk, troubleshoot or fix failing builds with Chunk, configure Chunk environments, schedule/proactivel (file: r6/chunk/SKILL.md)\n- circleci:circleci-builds: Diagnose and fix failing CircleCI builds quickly and safely. Use when users ask to investigate failed CircleCI jobs, triage flaky pipelines, identify root causes from logs, and implement minimal fixes in configuration, test setup, or build-rela (file: r6/builds/SKILL.md)\n- circleci:circleci-cli: Operate and troubleshoot CircleCI using the CircleCI CLI. Use when users ask to authenticate CLI access, inspect pipeline/workflow/job status, validate configuration locally, rerun pipelines/jobs, trigger pipelines, or gather actionable diagnosti (file: r6/cli/SKILL.md)\n- circleci:circleci-config: Optimize CircleCI configuration for speed, reliability, and maintainability. Use when users ask to improve `.circleci/config.yml`, reduce CI runtime, tune caching/workspaces/parallelism, remove pipeline waste, or fix flaky pipeline behavior cau (file: r6/config/SKILL.md)\n- cloudflare:agents-sdk: Build AI agents on Cloudflare Workers using the Agents SDK. Load when creating stateful agents, durable workflows, real-time WebSocket apps, scheduled tasks, MCP servers, or chat applications. Covers Agent class, state management, callable RPC (file: r7/agents-sdk/SKILL.md)\n- cloudflare:building-ai-agent-on-cloudflare: Builds AI agents on Cloudflare using the Agents SDK with state management, real-time WebSockets, scheduled tasks, tool integration, and chat capabilities. Generates production-ready agent code deployed to Workers. Use when: user wants to \"build  (file: r7/building-ai-agent-on-cloudflare/SKILL.md)\n- cloudflare:building-mcp-server-on-cloudflare: Builds remote MCP (Model Context Protocol) servers on Cloudflare Workers with tools, OAuth authentication, and production deployment. Generates server code, configures auth providers, and deploys to Workers. Use when: user wants to \"build MCP se (file: r7/building-mcp-server-on-cloudflare/SKILL.md)\n- cloudflare:cloudflare: Comprehensive Cloudflare platform skill covering Workers, Pages, storage (KV, D1, R2), AI (Workers AI, Vectorize, Agents SDK), networking (Tunnel, Spectrum), security (WAF, DDoS), and infrastructure-as-code (Terraform, Pulumi). Use for any Clo (file: r7/cloudflare/SKILL.md)\n- cloudflare:durable-objects: Create and review Cloudflare Durable Objects. Use when building stateful coordination (chat rooms, multiplayer games, booking systems), implementing RPC methods, SQLite storage, alarms, WebSockets, or reviewing DO code for best practices. Covers (file: r7/durable-objects/SKILL.md)\n- cloudflare:sandbox-sdk: Build sandboxed applications for secure code execution. Load when building AI code execution, code interpreters, CI/CD systems, interactive dev environments, or executing untrusted code. Covers Sandbox SDK lifecycle, commands, files, code interp (file: r7/sandbox-sdk/SKILL.md)\n- cloudflare:web-perf: Analyzes web performance using Chrome DevTools MCP. Measures Core Web Vitals (FCP, LCP, TBT, CLS, Speed Index), identifies render-blocking resources, network dependency chains, layout shifts, caching issues, and accessibility gaps. Use when as (file: r7/web-perf/SKILL.md)\n- cloudflare:workers-best-practices: Reviews and authors Cloudflare Workers code against production best practices. Load when writing new Workers, reviewing Worker code, configuring wrangler.jsonc, or checking for common Workers anti-patterns (streaming, floating promises, global (file: r7/workers-best-practices/SKILL.md)\n- cloudflare:wrangler: Cloudflare Workers CLI for deploying, developing, and managing Workers, KV, R2, D1, Vectorize, Hyperdrive, Workers AI, Containers, Queues, Workflows, Pipelines, and Secrets Store. Load before running wrangler commands to ensure correct syntax  (file: r7/wrangler/SKILL.md)\n- coderabbit:code-review: Reviews code changes using CodeRabbit AI. Use when user asks for code review, PR feedback, code quality checks, security issues, or requests fix-review cycles. (file: r8/coderabbit/63976030/skills/coderabbit-review/SKILL.md)\n- computer-use:computer-use: Control local Mac apps through Computer Use. Use for tasks that require reading or operating app UI by clicking, typing, scrolling, dragging, pressing keys, or setting values. (file: r2/computer-use/1.0.780/skills/computer-use/SKILL.md)\n- documents:documents: Create, edit, redline, and comment on `.docx`, Word, and Google Docs-targeted document artifacts inside the container, with a strict render-and-verify workflow. Use `render_docx.py` to generate page PNGs (and optional PDF) for visual QA, then  (file: r13/documents/26.515.10143/skills/documents/SKILL.md)\n- game-studio:game-playtest: Run browser-game playtests and frontend QA. Use when the user asks for smoke tests, screenshot-based verification, browser automation, HUD or overlay review, or structured issue-finding in a browser game. (file: r9/game-playtest/SKILL.md)\n- game-studio:game-studio: Route early browser-game work. Use when the user needs stack selection and workflow planning across design, implementation, assets, and playtesting before moving to a specialist skill. (file: r9/game-studio/SKILL.md)\n- game-studio:game-ui-frontend: Design UI surfaces for browser games. Use when the user asks for HUDs, menus, overlays, responsive layouts, or visual direction that must protect the playfield. (file: r9/game-ui-frontend/SKILL.md)\n- game-studio:phaser-2d-game: Implement 2D browser games with Phaser. Use when the user wants a Phaser, TypeScript, and Vite stack for scenes, gameplay systems, cameras, sprite animation, and DOM-overlay HUD patterns. (file: r9/phaser-2d-game/SKILL.md)\n- game-studio:react-three-fiber-game: Build React-hosted 3D browser games with React Three Fiber. Use when the user wants pmndrs-based scene composition, shared React state, and 3D HUD integration inside a React app. (file: r9/react-three-fiber-game/SKILL.md)\n- game-studio:sprite-pipeline: Generate and normalize 2D sprite animations. Use when the user asks for full-strip generation from approved source frames, consistent anchor and scale normalization, or preview assets for browser-game animation. (file: r9/sprite-pipeline/SKILL.md)\n- game-studio:three-webgl-game: Implement browser-game runtimes with plain Three.js. Use when the user wants imperative scene control in TypeScript or Vite with GLB assets, loaders, physics, and low-level WebGL debugging. (file: r9/three-webgl-game/SKILL.md)\n- game-studio:web-3d-asset-pipeline: Prepare and optimize browser-game 3D assets. Use when the user asks for GLB or glTF shipping work, including Blender cleanup and export, collision or LOD setup, compression, texture packaging, and runtime validation. (file: r9/web-3d-asset-pipeline/SKILL.md)\n- game-studio:web-game-foundations: Set browser-game architecture before implementation. Use when the user needs engine choice, simulation and render boundaries, input model, asset organization, or save/debug/performance strategy. (file: r9/web-game-foundations/SKILL.md)\n- github:gh-address-comments: Address actionable GitHub pull request review feedback. Use when the user wants to inspect unresolved review threads, requested changes, or inline review comments on a PR, then implement selected fixes. Use the GitHub app for PR metadata and fl (file: r10/gh-address-comments/SKILL.md)\n- github:gh-fix-ci: Use when a user asks to debug or fix failing GitHub PR checks that run in GitHub Actions. Use the GitHub app from this plugin for PR metadata and patch context, and use `gh` for Actions check and log inspection before implementing any approved  (file: r10/gh-fix-ci/SKILL.md)\n- github:github: Triage and orient GitHub repository, pull request, and issue work through the connected GitHub app. Use when the user asks for general GitHub help, wants PR or issue summaries, or needs repository context before choosing a more specific GitHub wo (file: r10/github/SKILL.md)\n- github:yeet: Publish local changes to GitHub by confirming scope, committing intentionally, pushing the branch, and opening a draft PR through the GitHub app from this plugin, with `gh` used only as a fallback where connector coverage is insufficient. (file: r10/yeet/SKILL.md)\n- imagegen: Generate or edit raster images when a task needs AI-created bitmap visuals such as character cards, illustrations, textures, sprites, mockups, photos, or transparent-background cutouts. Use when creating new images, transforming existing images,  (file: r0/imagegen/SKILL.md)\n- presentations:Presentations: Build premium editorial analytics presentation decks for PowerPoint and Google Slides with artifact-tool presentation JSX, using ruthless narrative editing, chart-first storytelling, rendered critique, platform-specific delivery rules, and ite (file: r13/presentations/26.515.10143/skills/presentations/SKILL.md)\n- remotion-best-practices: Best practices for Remotion - Video creation in React (file: r0/remotion/SKILL.md)\n- spreadsheets:Spreadsheets: Use this skill when a user requests to create, modify, analyze, visualize, or work with spreadsheet files (`.xlsx`, `.xls`, `.csv`, `.tsv`) or Google Sheets-targeted spreadsheet artifacts with formulas, formatting, charts, tables, and recalcul (file: r13/spreadsheets/26.515.10143/skills/spreadsheets/SKILL.md)\n- stripe:stripe-best-practices: Guides Stripe integration decisions — API selection (Checkout Sessions vs PaymentIntents), Connect platform setup (Accounts v2, controller properties), billing/subscriptions, Treasury financial accounts, integration surfaces (Checkout, Payment El (file: r11/stripe-best-practices/SKILL.md)\n- stripe:upgrade-stripe: Guide for upgrading Stripe API versions and SDKs (file: r11/upgrade-stripe/SKILL.md)\n- test-android-apps:android-emulator-qa: Use when validating Android feature flows in an emulator with adb-driven launch, input, UI-tree inspection, screenshots, and logcat capture. (file: r12/android-emulator-qa/SKILL.md)\n- test-android-apps:android-performance: Gather and interpret Android performance evidence on an adb target using Simpleperf CPU profiles, Perfetto or Compose traces, gfxinfo frame data, dumpsys meminfo snapshots, Java heap dumps, and native allocation traces. Use when asked to profile (file: r12/android-performance/SKILL.md)\n### How to use skills\n- Discovery: The list above is the skills available in this session (name + description + short path). Skill bodies live on disk at the listed paths after expanding the matching alias from `### Skill roots`.\n- Trigger rules: If the user names a skill (with `$SkillName` or plain text) OR the task clearly matches a skill's description shown above, you must use that skill for that turn. Multiple mentions mean use them all. Do not carry skills across turns unless re-mentioned.\n- Missing/blocked: If a named skill isn't in the list or the path can't be read, say so briefly and continue with the best fallback.\n- How to use a skill (progressive disclosure):\n  1) After deciding to use a skill, expand the listed short `path` with the matching alias from `### Skill roots`, then open its `SKILL.md`. Read only enough to follow the workflow.\n  2) When `SKILL.md` references relative paths (e.g., `scripts/foo.py`), resolve them relative to the directory containing that expanded `SKILL.md` first, and only consider other paths if needed.\n  3) If `SKILL.md` points to extra folders such as `references/`, load only the specific files needed for the request; don't bulk-load everything.\n  4) If `scripts/` exist, prefer running or patching them instead of retyping large code blocks.\n  5) If `assets/` or templates exist, reuse them instead of recreating from scratch.\n- Coordination and sequencing:\n  - If multiple skills apply, choose the minimal set that covers the request and state the order you'll use them.\n  - Announce which skill(s) you're using and why (one short line). If you skip an obvious skill, say why.\n- Context hygiene:\n  - Keep context small: summarize long sections instead of pasting them; only load extra files when needed.\n  - Avoid deep reference-chasing: prefer opening only files directly linked from `SKILL.md` unless you're blocked.\n  - When variants exist (frameworks, providers, domains), pick only the relevant reference file(s) and note that choice.\n- Safety and fallback: If a skill can't be applied cleanly (missing files, unclear instructions), state the issue, pick the next-best approach, and continue.\n</skills_instructions>"},{"type":"input_text","text":"<plugins_instructions>\n## Plugins\nA plugin is a local bundle of skills, MCP servers, and apps. Below is the list of plugins that are enabled and available in this session.\n### Available plugins\n- `Browser`: Browser / browser-use plugin Aliases: @browser, @browser-use, browser-use, Browser, in-app browser. Use Browser, the Codex in-app browser, when the user asks to open, inspect, navigate, test, click, type, or screenshot local web targets such as localhost, 127.0.0.1, ::1, file:// URLs, or the current in-app browser tab. After significant frontend changes to a local app, use Browser to open the relevant local target when it is known or obvious, unless the user asks for another browser tool. For requests like \"open localhost:3000\" or \"open to localhost:4000\", navigate the in-app browser to http://localhost:3000 or http://localhost:4000. Do not satisfy explicit @browser or @browser-use requests with macOS `open`, shell commands, or generic web browsing unless the user asks for another browser tool or approves a fallback.\n- `Build iOS Apps`: Build iOS apps with workflows for App Intents, SwiftUI UI work, refactors, performance profiling, leak investigation, and simulator debugging.\n- `Build macOS Apps`: Build, run, test, debug, instrument, and implement local macOS apps using Xcode, SwiftUI, AppKit interop, unified logging, and shell-first desktop workflows.\n- `Build Web Apps`: Build web apps with frontend asset design, browser testing, UI components, payments, and database guidance.\n- `CircleCI`: Build, test, and deploy any application\n- `Cloudflare`: Cloudflare platform plugin with curated skills for Workers, Wrangler, and Agents SDK plus the official Cloudflare API MCP server.\n- `CodeRabbit`: AI-powered code review in Codex, powered by CodeRabbit.\n- `Computer Use`: Control desktop apps on macOS from Codex through Computer Use.\n- `Documents`: Create and edit document artifacts in Codex, including Word files and Google Docs.\n- `Game Studio`: Design, prototype, and ship browser games with guided 2D and 3D workflows, asset pipelines, and playtesting support.\n- `GitHub`: Inspect repositories, triage pull requests and issues, debug CI, and publish changes through a hybrid GitHub connector and CLI workflow.\n- `Presentations`: Create, edit, render, verify, and export presentation slide decks. Use when Codex needs to build or modify a deck, slidedeck, presentation deck, slide deck, slides, PowerPoint, Google Slides, PPT, PPTX, .ppt, or .pptx file.\n- `Spreadsheets`: Create, edit, analyze, visualize, render, and export spreadsheets or Google Sheets-ready workbooks in Codex.\n- `Stripe`: Payments and business tools\n- `Test Android Apps`: Test Android apps with emulator workflows for reproduction, screenshots, UI inspection, log capture, and performance profiling.\n### How to use plugins\n- Discovery: The list above is the plugins available in this session.\n- Skill naming: If a plugin contributes skills, those skill entries are prefixed with `plugin_name:` in the Skills list.\n- Trigger rules: If the user explicitly names a plugin, prefer capabilities associated with that plugin for that turn.\n- Relationship to capabilities: Plugins are not invoked directly. Use their underlying skills, MCP tools, and app tools to help solve the task.\n- Preference: When a relevant plugin is available, prefer using capabilities associated with that plugin over standalone capabilities that provide similar functionality.\n- Missing/blocked: If the user requests a plugin that is not listed above, or the plugin does not have relevant callable capabilities for the task, say so briefly and continue with the best fallback.\n</plugins_instructions>"}]},{"type":"message","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"<environment_context>\n  <cwd>/Users/vincet/Documents/aiglass</cwd>\n  <shell>zsh</shell>\n  <current_date>2026-05-17</current_date>\n  <timezone>Asia/Taipei</timezone>\n</environment_context>"}]},{"type":"message","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"按这个要求，制作安卓版本apk\n"}]},{"type":"compaction","encrypted_content":"gAAAAABqCTQRrVlaL0RQBOA5-Mz0gQN-DzwTuzVBSO1eSoul7ZQNasZ3OjRuWb3W7z-8gwpJ-tZqjrPmCY7lP2_JBPF22u3o8k6bVxwYnbM1rsTOsa4UDyD5fMyqiCuISq6CGGbI5AsrUVwJTOuEwp3v_82mzPsfs2Xo9bSM9bhr6RoXuskdKAypWoUYH1J7IF5nqf1W1Wxfjrj4UdKHU8hPeDjVktr7CEgTCqtauGTgDpHhmrxkQIxWx9nM4AQyTP1nh1QQf7VJT_rCBLFSlOiehls89pz8N06qJ-OOv50ZIBAJXIXTGe7pyxjc4DEmQDdI8OjABFgiGO1eIu9MEqleJ5PuQJ8LcWyUvLA9o3IfHSzov-O00KYaIRq7u3n_WpWb8JnyaxLyg21XGQkzyYtE8_lHPQ4MBUhZqgIotcfTnY_M5nPePCdDbiuQ0l8toiaDZCzQEqUXR-sSb8GZxn6-T3WdULdE0Vt_JToKPYZRDqukujB3pQGvUS8pzqowBh76